00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:09.270
And now, for my discussion
with Dr. David Spiegel,

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:11.200
David, thank you so much for being here.

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:12.373
- Andrew, my pleasure.

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:17.333
- Can you tell us, what is hypnosis?

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:21.920
- Hypnosis is a state of
highly focused attention.

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:23.050
It's something like looking

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:26.120
through the telephoto lens
of a camera in consciousness,

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:27.940
what you see, you see with great detail,

00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:30.323
but devoid of context.

00:09:31.210 --> 00:09:32.300
If you've had the experience

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:33.840
of getting so caught up in a good movie,

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:35.420
that you forget you're watching a movie

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:36.770
and a enter the imaginary world.

00:09:36.770 --> 00:09:38.860
You're part of the movie,
not part of the audience,

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:41.840
you're experiencing it,
you're not evaluating it.

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:43.520
That's a hypnotic like experience

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:46.250
that many people have
in their everyday lives.

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:49.950
- So, is any experience
that really draws us in,

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:51.960
hypnotic in that sense?

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:55.860
Or let me give a different example.

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:58.630
If I'm watching a sports game

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:00.720
and I'm really wrapped up in the game,

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:03.750
but I'm also in touch with how
it makes me feel in my body

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:07.910
kind of registering the
excitement or the anticipation.

00:10:07.910 --> 00:10:10.070
Is that a state of hypnosis also,

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:12.040
because you mentioned there's kind of,

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:14.030
a narrowing of context.
- Right.

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:16.000
- But a kind of losing of the self,

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:17.880
or is it, do I have that right?

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:20.701
- Yes, it is true that you're-

00:10:20.701 --> 00:10:22.810
[indistinct] to the extent
that you're somatic,

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:27.190
your body experience is
a part of the sport event

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:28.690
that you're engaged with.

00:10:28.690 --> 00:10:32.660
I'd say that is a self
altering hypnotic experience.

00:10:32.660 --> 00:10:35.570
If your physical reactions
are distracting you

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:38.820
or make you think about something else,

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:41.710
that's when it's less hypnotic like,

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:44.380
and more, just one of a
series of experiences.

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:45.460
- Okay.

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:47.130
So I have to ask,

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:50.300
how did you get into this
business of hypnosis?

00:10:50.300 --> 00:10:51.878
Because,

00:10:51.878 --> 00:10:52.990
I think for most people,

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:55.790
when they hear hypnosis or
they think about hypnosis,

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:57.750
they think of stage hypnosis.
- Right.

00:10:57.750 --> 00:11:00.140
- They think of somebody with
a pendant going back and forth

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:02.483
or people up on a stage,

00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:07.550
behaving abnormally for the
entertainment of others.

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:09.130
- Yeah.

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:11.120
- How did you get into hypnosis

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:14.220
as an interest, as a practice?

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:15.860
And if you would,

00:11:15.860 --> 00:11:18.960
could you contrast the sort
of hypnosis that you do

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:21.360
in the clinical setting
with a sort of hypnosis

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:22.940
that a stage hypnotist does?

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:24.070
- Sure.

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:28.780
Well, it is something of a
genetic illness in my family.

00:11:28.780 --> 00:11:32.500
Both of my parents were
psychiatrists and psychoanalysts

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:33.670
and they told me I was free

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:35.960
to be any kind of
psychiatrist I wanted to be.

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:37.623
So, here I am.

00:11:38.570 --> 00:11:43.233
My father was training to
be a psychoanalyst in 1943.

00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:46.600
And,

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:49.950
he ran into a Vietnamese refugee,

00:11:49.950 --> 00:11:51.990
who couldn't serve in the army,

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:54.450
but who had studied hypnosis.

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:56.480
And actually it would interest you,

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:58.570
doing your ophthalmological research.

00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:02.040
He had a smallpox scar right
in the middle of his forehead,

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:03.840
and he did forensic examinations.

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:06.010
And he noticed that some of the prisoners,

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:08.750
would focus on that spot on his forehead

00:12:08.750 --> 00:12:12.000
and then close their eyes
and seem to go to sleep.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.030
But they were in some altered state.

00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:15.740
So he got interested in hypnosis.

00:12:15.740 --> 00:12:18.340
He used it forensically

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:21.530
and his name was Gustav von Aschaffenburg.

00:12:21.530 --> 00:12:25.250
And he offered to teach
young psychiatrists,

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:29.520
how to use hypnosis when
they went off into the war.

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:31.950
And so he trained my father

00:12:31.950 --> 00:12:36.030
and my got off the
analytic couch and asked,

00:12:36.030 --> 00:12:37.670
the analyst mentioned it to him.

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:39.090
That's how he found out about it.

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:41.520
And my father said, did I say
something wrong in analysis?

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:43.760
Why is he talking to me [laughs] now?

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:47.040
And he found it very
useful in helping soldiers

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:49.250
who had acute pain when they were wounded

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:50.830
and helping people with conversion

00:12:50.830 --> 00:12:52.980
in post-traumatic stress disorders.

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:56.490
And when he came back, he
went back to his training,

00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:59.450
but he still was sort of interested in it.

00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:01.950
And he had his, one of his supervisors

00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:02.930
was Frieda Fromm-Reichmann,

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:05.550
who was a very famous psychoanalyst.

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:09.360
And he said that he had been
told to stop doing hypnosis

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:12.160
because it would ruin his
reputation as an analyst.

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:13.230
And she said to him,

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:15.670
what are you so worried of
about your reputation for?

00:13:15.670 --> 00:13:18.440
You're going to give a course
at the institute in hypnosis,

00:13:18.440 --> 00:13:20.560
and I know you're going to do
it because I'm going to take it.

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:23.710
So he was teaching Frieda
Fromm-Reichmann hypnosis,

00:13:23.710 --> 00:13:25.000
and he just kept doing it.

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:25.833
And after a while,

00:13:25.833 --> 00:13:28.560
he discovered that he was
getting better results

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:30.230
with a few sessions of hypnosis

00:13:30.230 --> 00:13:34.240
than he was with daily
psychoanalysis with his patients.

00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:36.580
And so he switched his practice.

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:38.250
And so the dinner table conversations,

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:39.590
were pretty interesting.

00:13:39.590 --> 00:13:42.450
And occasionally when he was
making a movie of a patient,

00:13:42.450 --> 00:13:44.280
I would get to watch that.

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:47.950
And so, when I went to medical school,

00:13:47.950 --> 00:13:49.172
I figured I'll take a course.

00:13:49.172 --> 00:13:50.210
[mumbles] Tom Hackett,

00:13:50.210 --> 00:13:52.560
who was a chair of psychiatry
of mass general was teaching

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:54.790
and it was a very interesting course.

00:13:54.790 --> 00:13:58.000
And the day that converted me was,

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:01.270
I was doing my rotation at
Children's Hospital in Boston.

00:14:01.270 --> 00:14:02.720
And the nurse is telling me,

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:04.890
Spiegel, your next patient is an asthmatic

00:14:04.890 --> 00:14:06.840
in room 437 or something.

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:07.730
And I'm just following,

00:14:07.730 --> 00:14:09.730
the sound of the wheezes down the hall.

00:14:09.730 --> 00:14:11.770
I go in the room, there's
a 16 year old girl,

00:14:11.770 --> 00:14:14.740
knuckles white, bolt upright
in bed, struggling for breath.

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:16.110
You can hear the wheezing.

00:14:16.110 --> 00:14:18.750
She twice had subcutaneous
epinephrine, didn't work.

00:14:18.750 --> 00:14:20.520
They were thinking
about general anesthesia

00:14:20.520 --> 00:14:22.510
and starting her on steroids.

00:14:22.510 --> 00:14:25.270
And her mother's there crying.

00:14:25.270 --> 00:14:28.100
And I said, I don't know what else to do.

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:30.260
So I said, you want to
learn a breathing exercise?

00:14:30.260 --> 00:14:31.640
And she nods.

00:14:31.640 --> 00:14:33.580
And I got her hypnotized.

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:35.210
And then I realized we
hadn't gotten to asthma

00:14:35.210 --> 00:14:36.043
in the course yet.

00:14:36.043 --> 00:14:37.900
So, I made up something very complex.

00:14:37.900 --> 00:14:40.360
I said, each breath you
take will be a little deeper

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:42.110
and a little easier.

00:14:42.110 --> 00:14:44.180
And within five minutes,
she's lying back in bed.

00:14:44.180 --> 00:14:45.970
Her knuckles aren't
white, she's not wheezing.

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:47.640
Her mother stopped crying.

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:49.480
The nurse ran out of the room,

00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:51.610
and the intern, my intern comes to find me

00:14:51.610 --> 00:14:53.450
and I figured he is going to
pat me on the back and say,

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:54.960
nice job, Spiegel.

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:56.610
He said, the nurse has filed a complaint

00:14:56.610 --> 00:14:57.910
with a nursing supervisor

00:14:57.910 --> 00:15:00.960
that you violated a
Massachusetts law by hypnotizing,

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:02.923
a minor without parental consent.

00:15:03.850 --> 00:15:07.460
And I thought, oh, that's nice.

00:15:07.460 --> 00:15:09.690
I doubt there is a law like this.

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:10.650
So, the interns says,

00:15:10.650 --> 00:15:12.400
you're going to have to
stop doing this with her.

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:13.233
And I said, why?

00:15:13.233 --> 00:15:14.210
He said, it's dangerous.

00:15:14.210 --> 00:15:16.580
I said, you're going to
give her general anesthesia

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:17.460
and put her on steroids,

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:19.063
and talking to her is dangerous?

00:15:19.898 --> 00:15:21.030
He said, well, you'll have to do it.

00:15:21.030 --> 00:15:22.180
And I said, I'll tell you what,

00:15:22.180 --> 00:15:23.820
take me off the case if you want,

00:15:23.820 --> 00:15:25.410
but I'm not going to
tell a patient of mine,

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:27.350
anything I know is not true.

00:15:27.350 --> 00:15:30.527
So, there was a battle over
the weekend about what to do,

00:15:30.527 --> 00:15:33.470
and the intern, the chief
resident, the attending,

00:15:33.470 --> 00:15:34.800
were all arguing about it.

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:37.990
And on Monday they came
back with a radical idea.

00:15:37.990 --> 00:15:40.350
They said, let's ask the patient.

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:41.780
I don't think this has ever been done

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:43.270
at children's [chuckles] hospital before.

00:15:43.270 --> 00:15:45.060
And she said, oh, I like this.

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:46.480
She'd been hospitalized every month

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:48.670
for three months in status asthmaticus,

00:15:48.670 --> 00:15:51.530
she did a one subsequent
hospitalization but after that,

00:15:51.530 --> 00:15:54.220
went on to study to be
a respiratory therapist.

00:15:54.220 --> 00:15:55.510
And I thought that anything,

00:15:55.510 --> 00:15:57.040
that can help a patient that much,

00:15:57.040 --> 00:15:59.890
violate a nonexistent Massachusetts law,

00:15:59.890 --> 00:16:01.480
frustrate the nursing supervisor,

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:02.910
had to be worth looking into.

00:16:02.910 --> 00:16:04.870
So, I just kept doing it.

00:16:04.870 --> 00:16:06.690
I discovered that there were,

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:08.930
all of my classmates in medical school,

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:10.810
had just read the new issue
of the New England Journal

00:16:10.810 --> 00:16:13.240
and had some new medication to suggest.

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:14.210
And I would, you know,

00:16:14.210 --> 00:16:15.760
surgeons would say, look,

00:16:15.760 --> 00:16:18.860
if you can help this guy
with his pain or his anxiety,

00:16:18.860 --> 00:16:21.170
anything above the neck,
that's yours, do it, Spiegel.

00:16:21.170 --> 00:16:24.590
So, I was having fun and being able

00:16:24.590 --> 00:16:26.560
to learn how to help people

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:28.990
in a way that just otherwise
was not being done.

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:32.530
And so, it got me thinking
about the fact that,

00:16:32.530 --> 00:16:33.570
we're born with this brain

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:35.380
but we don't have a user's manual for it.

00:16:35.380 --> 00:16:37.710
And we don't use it
nearly as well as we can.

00:16:37.710 --> 00:16:40.530
And that's something your
research is all about too.

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:42.374
And so, I thought,

00:16:42.374 --> 00:16:44.130
I want to understand this better.

00:16:44.130 --> 00:16:45.810
And I want to see what we can do.

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:47.870
Stage hypnotists drive me nuts.

00:16:47.870 --> 00:16:49.883
They make fools out of people.

00:16:50.915 --> 00:16:51.983
There was one.

00:16:53.936 --> 00:16:55.260
This is a case my father was involved.

00:16:55.260 --> 00:16:57.690
He got a call from, he was
at Columbia, he got a call,

00:16:57.690 --> 00:17:00.460
Spiegel, you got to come see
this woman, she's in the ER.

00:17:00.460 --> 00:17:05.430
And she's in some kind of weird
upset state that happened.

00:17:05.430 --> 00:17:07.400
And it turned out she'd been on the show

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:09.980
with a stage hypnotist who,

00:17:09.980 --> 00:17:11.520
and what they do by the way is,

00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:13.760
they cycle around, you know,

00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:14.630
The beginning of the show,

00:17:14.630 --> 00:17:17.040
they don't just grab somebody
and say, we're doing this.

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:18.440
They get a bunch of people up.

00:17:18.440 --> 00:17:20.820
They do what [indistinct] testing,

00:17:20.820 --> 00:17:21.653
to see if people-

00:17:21.653 --> 00:17:24.550
and they get the ones who
are the most hypnotizable.

00:17:24.550 --> 00:17:26.400
So she was the one.

00:17:26.400 --> 00:17:30.480
And he said, there's now a
little bird in your hand,

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:32.530
and you're going to play with the bird.

00:17:32.530 --> 00:17:35.590
And she starts to cry and scream.

00:17:35.590 --> 00:17:37.630
And he just gets her off the stage,

00:17:37.630 --> 00:17:39.220
'cause it's very upsetting.

00:17:39.220 --> 00:17:40.950
And she's wandering around New York City

00:17:40.950 --> 00:17:43.610
in the middle of the night dissociated

00:17:43.610 --> 00:17:45.070
and brought to Columbia.

00:17:45.070 --> 00:17:46.230
And that's where my father saw her.

00:17:46.230 --> 00:17:48.840
She was still in a kind of
uncomfortable trance like state.

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:49.883
And it turned out,

00:17:49.883 --> 00:17:53.140
that she was the trophy
wife of a very wealthy guy.

00:17:53.140 --> 00:17:56.080
And she felt like a bird in a gilded cage.

00:17:56.080 --> 00:17:57.190
And so to her,

00:17:57.190 --> 00:18:01.270
that image just triggered
all of this sense of,

00:18:01.270 --> 00:18:04.740
dissatisfaction and discomfort,
fear about her life.

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:07.510
And he was able to get her
reoriented and talk with her

00:18:07.510 --> 00:18:09.480
about what she was going to of her life.

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:11.330
But I don't like stage hypnosis.

00:18:11.330 --> 00:18:13.000
You're making fools out of people

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:14.590
and you're using the fact,

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:16.360
and that's what scares
people about hypnosis.

00:18:16.360 --> 00:18:18.200
They think you're losing control.

00:18:18.200 --> 00:18:19.170
You're gaining control.

00:18:19.170 --> 00:18:22.010
Self-hypnosis is a way of
enhancing your control,

00:18:22.010 --> 00:18:24.120
over your mind and your body.

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:25.640
It can work very well,

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:29.270
but because it gives you a
kind of cognitive flexibility,

00:18:29.270 --> 00:18:32.620
you're able to shift sets very easily.

00:18:32.620 --> 00:18:36.280
To give up judging and
evaluating the way you usually do

00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:38.750
and see something from a
different point of view.

00:18:38.750 --> 00:18:40.990
That's a great therapeutic opportunity,

00:18:40.990 --> 00:18:43.380
but if misused, it could be a danger too,

00:18:43.380 --> 00:18:45.420
and that's what scares people about it.

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:48.820
It is that very ability to
suspend critical judgment

00:18:48.820 --> 00:18:51.330
and just have an experience
and see what happens.

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:53.860
That can be a great
therapeutic opportunity,

00:18:53.860 --> 00:18:55.290
but if somebody's misusing it,

00:18:55.290 --> 00:18:56.550
it can be a way to harm people.

00:18:56.550 --> 00:19:00.110
And there are plenty
of examples of people,

00:19:00.110 --> 00:19:02.020
having fantasies imposed on them,

00:19:02.020 --> 00:19:03.580
that they come to think are realities.

00:19:03.580 --> 00:19:05.250
It's not unusual these days.

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:06.083
So,

00:19:06.940 --> 00:19:08.530
it's an ability that,

00:19:08.530 --> 00:19:11.350
if people learn to
recognize and understand it,

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:13.323
can be a tremendous therapeutic tool.

00:19:14.380 --> 00:19:16.830
- I've been stage hypnotized

00:19:18.130 --> 00:19:22.340
and I've been clinically
hypnotized many times

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:24.650
through a self-hypnosis app.

00:19:24.650 --> 00:19:25.963
We'll talk about later.

00:19:27.232 --> 00:19:30.070
And then I know we have plans
for you to hypnotize me today.

00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:31.960
You've done it once before.
- Mm-hm.

00:19:31.960 --> 00:19:33.650
- [Andrew] And I'm very hypnotizable

00:19:33.650 --> 00:19:35.570
as we both know.
- Right.

00:19:35.570 --> 00:19:36.403
- We'll talk about,

00:19:36.403 --> 00:19:38.830
how one can gauge their hypnotizability.

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:40.913
- Sure.
- But the stage hypnosis,

00:19:41.900 --> 00:19:42.800
was interesting.

00:19:42.800 --> 00:19:43.940
This was in college,

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:45.670
they brought someone out to the dormitory

00:19:45.670 --> 00:19:47.070
and,

00:19:47.070 --> 00:19:49.900
I recall being one of the people

00:19:49.900 --> 00:19:51.353
that was selected.
- Mm-hm.

00:19:54.446 --> 00:19:57.910
- And engaging in very
bizarre behavior, right?

00:19:57.910 --> 00:19:59.350
It wasn't thoroughly embarrassing,

00:19:59.350 --> 00:20:00.930
but it was pretty embarrassing.

00:20:00.930 --> 00:20:03.350
And then being sent off the stage,

00:20:03.350 --> 00:20:05.150
and as I was exiting,

00:20:05.150 --> 00:20:07.010
suddenly screaming something out

00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:09.920
because he had planted a
suggestion of some sort.

00:20:09.920 --> 00:20:11.420
- Ah, mm-hm.
- And then I was told

00:20:11.420 --> 00:20:13.060
to look in my pocket and there was,

00:20:13.060 --> 00:20:14.950
like a, I think, a torn up dollar bill.

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:15.870
There were a bunch of things

00:20:15.870 --> 00:20:17.377
that I have vague recollection of.

00:20:17.377 --> 00:20:21.320
- Mm-hm.
But it raises set of questions

00:20:22.870 --> 00:20:25.293
that really boil down to,

00:20:26.350 --> 00:20:28.150
as a biologist, I always think that,

00:20:29.170 --> 00:20:32.080
there's no events in the
brain, there are processes.

00:20:32.080 --> 00:20:35.150
And so hypnosis, we know has an induction.

00:20:35.150 --> 00:20:37.210
Then one is hypnotized, I imagine.

00:20:37.210 --> 00:20:38.507
And then it sounds like this woman

00:20:38.507 --> 00:20:41.100
and this example of the bird

00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:44.510
and being distraught in New York City,

00:20:44.510 --> 00:20:47.740
is a failure to exit the hypnotic state.

00:20:47.740 --> 00:20:51.420
Do we know what sorts of
brain areas are active

00:20:51.420 --> 00:20:52.883
during the induction,

00:20:54.020 --> 00:20:55.430
let's call it the deep hypnosis

00:20:55.430 --> 00:20:57.190
and then what's shutting off

00:20:57.190 --> 00:20:59.730
or changing as people exit hypnosis.

00:20:59.730 --> 00:21:01.490
- Yes, we do, we've studied that.

00:21:01.490 --> 00:21:02.770
We've been very interested in that.

00:21:02.770 --> 00:21:07.490
And so, we did a study
where we selected highly

00:21:07.490 --> 00:21:09.670
and non-hypnotizable people

00:21:09.670 --> 00:21:11.610
so we could do the comparison.

00:21:11.610 --> 00:21:15.000
And then hypnotize them in
the functional MRI scanner.

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:18.670
And we found three things
characterized the entry

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:20.220
into the hypnotic state.

00:21:20.220 --> 00:21:22.130
The first is turning down activity

00:21:22.130 --> 00:21:24.170
in the dorsal anterior cingulate cortex.

00:21:24.170 --> 00:21:27.058
So the DACC is in the central
front-middle part of the brain

00:21:27.058 --> 00:21:28.840
as you well know.

00:21:28.840 --> 00:21:32.410
And it's part of what we
call the salience network.

00:21:32.410 --> 00:21:35.220
It's a conflict detector.

00:21:35.220 --> 00:21:40.160
So if you're engaged in work
and you hear a loud noise

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:41.550
that you think might be a gunshot,

00:21:41.550 --> 00:21:43.650
that's your anterior
cingulate cortex saying,

00:21:43.650 --> 00:21:44.530
hey, wait a minute,

00:21:44.530 --> 00:21:46.020
there's some potential danger over there.

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:47.270
You better pay attention to it.

00:21:47.270 --> 00:21:50.280
So, it compares what you're doing

00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:51.530
with what else is going on

00:21:51.530 --> 00:21:53.760
and helps you decide what to do.

00:21:53.760 --> 00:21:55.903
And as you can imagine,

00:21:56.830 --> 00:21:58.640
turning down activity in that region,

00:21:58.640 --> 00:22:00.630
make it less likely that
you'll be distracted

00:22:00.630 --> 00:22:02.810
and pulled out of whatever you're in.

00:22:02.810 --> 00:22:04.190
And in another study,

00:22:04.190 --> 00:22:06.040
we found that highly hypnotizable people,

00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:07.980
even without being hypnotized,

00:22:07.980 --> 00:22:12.170
have more functional
connectivity between the DACC,

00:22:12.170 --> 00:22:13.447
the anterior cingulate cortex

00:22:13.447 --> 00:22:17.080
and the left dorsolateral
prefrontal cortex.

00:22:17.080 --> 00:22:19.340
So, which is part of a key region

00:22:19.340 --> 00:22:20.850
in the executive control network.

00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:22.460
So, when you're engaging in tasks,

00:22:22.460 --> 00:22:24.670
you're enacting a plan,

00:22:24.670 --> 00:22:26.490
you're writing a paper, you're
doing whatever you're doing.

00:22:26.490 --> 00:22:29.940
That's the prefrontal
cortex who's doing that.

00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:31.970
And so, if that is coordinated,

00:22:31.970 --> 00:22:34.670
we found more functional connectivity.

00:22:34.670 --> 00:22:36.867
So, when one is up, the other's up

00:22:36.867 --> 00:22:38.550
and one is down, the other's down.

00:22:38.550 --> 00:22:41.890
That coordination implies
that the brain is saying,

00:22:41.890 --> 00:22:44.250
okay, go ahead, I know what you're doing.

00:22:44.250 --> 00:22:45.250
Carry out that plan.

00:22:45.250 --> 00:22:47.640
And don't worry about other possibilities.

00:22:47.640 --> 00:22:50.550
So, two other things happen
when people are hypnotized.

00:22:50.550 --> 00:22:54.100
One is that that DLPFC has
higher functional connectivity

00:22:54.100 --> 00:22:55.290
with the insula.

00:22:55.290 --> 00:22:57.060
Another part of the salience network.

00:22:57.060 --> 00:22:59.930
It's a part of the
mind-body control system,

00:22:59.930 --> 00:23:01.850
sensitive to what's happening in the body.

00:23:01.850 --> 00:23:04.160
It's part of the pain network as well,

00:23:04.160 --> 00:23:06.720
but it's also a region of the brain where,

00:23:06.720 --> 00:23:09.070
you can control things in your body

00:23:09.070 --> 00:23:10.300
that you wouldn't have think you could.

00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:12.670
For example, we did a study years ago,

00:23:12.670 --> 00:23:15.830
where we took people who
were highly hypnotizable,

00:23:15.830 --> 00:23:18.520
hypnotized them and told them,

00:23:18.520 --> 00:23:21.070
we went on a imaginary culinary tour.

00:23:21.070 --> 00:23:25.200
So, they would eat their
favorite foods and we found that,

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:29.410
they increased their gastric
acid secretion, like by 87%.

00:23:29.410 --> 00:23:32.530
So, their stomach was acting
as though it was about to get,

00:23:32.530 --> 00:23:33.730
I mean, there was one woman,

00:23:33.730 --> 00:23:36.040
it was so vivid for her that
halfway through, she said,

00:23:36.040 --> 00:23:38.710
let's stop, I'm full, [laughs]
eating these imaginary-

00:23:38.710 --> 00:23:40.017
- Having never eaten-
- Having never eaten anything-

00:23:40.017 --> 00:23:41.610
- Actual food?
- No.

00:23:41.610 --> 00:23:43.580
- Incredible.
- And then we got them

00:23:43.580 --> 00:23:46.800
to relax and think of
anything but food or drink.

00:23:46.800 --> 00:23:51.800
And we got like a 40% decrease
in gastric acid secretions.

00:23:52.070 --> 00:23:55.270
And that was DLPFC through the insula,

00:23:55.270 --> 00:23:56.850
telling the stomach you're getting food

00:23:56.850 --> 00:23:58.000
or you're not getting food.

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:00.520
And even, we injected
them with Pentagastrin,

00:24:00.520 --> 00:24:03.050
which triggers gastric acid release.

00:24:03.050 --> 00:24:04.930
And even then in the hypnosis condition,

00:24:04.930 --> 00:24:07.820
they had a 19% reduction in gastric acid.

00:24:07.820 --> 00:24:10.190
So, the brain has this amazing ability

00:24:10.190 --> 00:24:11.660
to control what's going on in the body

00:24:11.660 --> 00:24:14.430
in ways that we don't think
we have ability to control.

00:24:14.430 --> 00:24:15.920
That's just one example.

00:24:15.920 --> 00:24:18.490
So, that's the DLPFC insula connection.

00:24:18.490 --> 00:24:19.940
The third thing that happens,

00:24:19.940 --> 00:24:22.990
and this relates to what
you did on the stage,

00:24:22.990 --> 00:24:26.560
is you have inverse
functional connectivity

00:24:26.560 --> 00:24:31.010
between the DLPFC and the
posterior cingulate cortex.

00:24:31.010 --> 00:24:34.470
The posterior cingulate is part
of the default mode network.

00:24:34.470 --> 00:24:35.940
It's in the back of the brain.

00:24:35.940 --> 00:24:40.030
And it's an area whose
activity goes down for example,

00:24:40.030 --> 00:24:41.440
in meditators.

00:24:41.440 --> 00:24:43.720
And in meditation, you're
supposed to be selfless.

00:24:43.720 --> 00:24:45.320
You're supposed to, [indistinct]
self is an illusion,

00:24:45.320 --> 00:24:46.480
you're supposed to let it dissolve

00:24:46.480 --> 00:24:48.260
and just experience things.

00:24:48.260 --> 00:24:49.200
And when you're doing that,

00:24:49.200 --> 00:24:52.250
the posterior cingulate
is decreasing in activity,

00:24:52.250 --> 00:24:54.300
the inverse connection is,

00:24:54.300 --> 00:24:55.450
I'm doing something

00:24:55.450 --> 00:24:58.010
but I'm not thinking about
what it means for me.

00:24:58.010 --> 00:25:00.050
I may not even remember much of it.

00:25:00.050 --> 00:25:02.510
if I do, I don't care that much about it.

00:25:02.510 --> 00:25:05.710
And so, that is part of the dissociation

00:25:05.710 --> 00:25:06.950
that I occur with hypnosis.

00:25:06.950 --> 00:25:09.910
So, it's how you put things
outside of conscious awareness,

00:25:09.910 --> 00:25:11.560
and don't worry about what it means.

00:25:11.560 --> 00:25:14.390
It also adds to cognitive flexibility.

00:25:14.390 --> 00:25:15.620
If you're thinking,

00:25:15.620 --> 00:25:18.330
well, people like me
don't usually do this.

00:25:18.330 --> 00:25:20.730
That may inhibit you from,

00:25:20.730 --> 00:25:23.060
enacting a new form of
psychotherapy for example,

00:25:23.060 --> 00:25:25.130
that you've never done before.

00:25:25.130 --> 00:25:28.060
But if you're having
this decreased activity

00:25:28.060 --> 00:25:31.170
in the part of your brain that
reflects on what it means,

00:25:31.170 --> 00:25:33.430
you're more likely to
be cognitively flexible

00:25:33.430 --> 00:25:34.650
and willing to give it a try.

00:25:34.650 --> 00:25:36.640
And that's one of the
therapeutic advantages

00:25:36.640 --> 00:25:38.600
of hypnosis as well.

00:25:38.600 --> 00:25:41.240
- Fascinating, and it's really,

00:25:41.240 --> 00:25:43.612
I'm going to embarrass
you here a little bit.

00:25:43.612 --> 00:25:45.080
[David laughing]
In the positive sense,

00:25:45.080 --> 00:25:46.960
your laboratory is really the one

00:25:46.960 --> 00:25:49.750
that's pioneered brain
imaging of hypnotic states.

00:25:49.750 --> 00:25:51.680
And, it sounds like it,

00:25:51.680 --> 00:25:53.640
that's my understanding, is that correct?

00:25:53.640 --> 00:25:54.820
- Yeah, I mean, there are other people,

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:56.260
who've done excellent research too.

00:25:56.260 --> 00:25:58.510
- Sure.
Pierre Rainville in Montreal

00:25:58.510 --> 00:25:59.890
and several other people,

00:25:59.890 --> 00:26:01.638
but we're one of the leading labs

00:26:01.638 --> 00:26:04.270
in neuroimaging of hypnosis.

00:26:04.270 --> 00:26:06.180
- I have to ask about,

00:26:06.180 --> 00:26:08.660
attention deficit hyperactivity disorder.

00:26:08.660 --> 00:26:11.140
I get a lot of questions about this.

00:26:11.140 --> 00:26:12.760
And I think a lot of people just struggle

00:26:12.760 --> 00:26:15.010
with holding attention nowadays,

00:26:15.010 --> 00:26:18.980
because of interference
with phones and devices.

00:26:18.980 --> 00:26:21.250
And of course, there is a lot of,

00:26:21.250 --> 00:26:23.080
clinically legitimate ADHD out there,

00:26:23.080 --> 00:26:24.580
but the way that you describe,
- Sure.

00:26:24.580 --> 00:26:26.880
- the dorsal anterior cingulate

00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:30.550
and the salience network and
this a conflict detector of,

00:26:30.550 --> 00:26:33.500
am I focusing on something or
am I splitting my attention?

00:26:33.500 --> 00:26:34.960
How distractable am I?

00:26:34.960 --> 00:26:37.520
Seems to relate to some extent to activity

00:26:37.520 --> 00:26:40.740
in the anterior cingulate cortex.

00:26:40.740 --> 00:26:42.553
Do people with ADHD,

00:26:43.400 --> 00:26:46.500
display disruptions in
elements of these networks

00:26:46.500 --> 00:26:50.193
and has hypnosis ever been used to,

00:26:51.150 --> 00:26:53.080
or self-hypnosis, I should be,

00:26:53.080 --> 00:26:55.010
to distinguish from stage hypnosis,

00:26:55.010 --> 00:26:56.950
clinical and self-hypnosis been used

00:26:56.950 --> 00:26:59.170
to enhance people's ability to focus

00:26:59.170 --> 00:27:00.740
and hold attention?

00:27:00.740 --> 00:27:02.780
Because that's such a built in component

00:27:02.780 --> 00:27:03.983
of the hypnotic state.

00:27:05.170 --> 00:27:07.200
- It's a great question.

00:27:07.200 --> 00:27:08.890
There's sort of two
ways to think about it,

00:27:08.890 --> 00:27:11.070
in terms of enhancing focus, yes.

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:14.510
It has been very helpful
in teaching people

00:27:14.510 --> 00:27:16.110
to just prepare your mind

00:27:16.110 --> 00:27:18.200
to narrow in and focus on something.

00:27:18.200 --> 00:27:20.530
And when you're really
engaged in reading something,

00:27:20.530 --> 00:27:21.510
or you're writing a pa-

00:27:21.510 --> 00:27:22.390
I mean, I'll have that,

00:27:22.390 --> 00:27:23.223
sometimes I'm thinking,

00:27:23.223 --> 00:27:25.580
oh God, I have to do
this for another hour.

00:27:25.580 --> 00:27:27.880
Other times an hour will
go by and I'll think,

00:27:27.880 --> 00:27:30.010
hey, great, because when you're in that,

00:27:30.010 --> 00:27:31.660
it feels game-like to you,

00:27:31.660 --> 00:27:33.950
you're just assembling
the parts of the puzzle

00:27:33.950 --> 00:27:35.670
and putting 'em together.

00:27:35.670 --> 00:27:37.290
It's fun, you just get absorbed,

00:27:37.290 --> 00:27:39.470
that for me, that's a
hypnotic like experience,

00:27:39.470 --> 00:27:41.780
when I'm having trouble,
when I'm struggling,

00:27:41.780 --> 00:27:45.070
sometimes doing things like
self-hypnosis can help.

00:27:45.070 --> 00:27:47.680
I'm not an expert on ADHD.

00:27:47.680 --> 00:27:49.450
My impression is that you're right,

00:27:49.450 --> 00:27:52.300
that these are people who
are constantly distracted

00:27:52.300 --> 00:27:53.730
and rather rigid.

00:27:53.730 --> 00:27:55.790
The other part of it is
they're easily distractable.

00:27:55.790 --> 00:27:58.380
They're very upset when
they get distracted

00:27:58.380 --> 00:28:00.357
and they're rather rigid in
what they want to attend to

00:28:00.357 --> 00:28:01.290
and what they can.

00:28:01.290 --> 00:28:03.150
I think, as a way of controlling this,

00:28:03.150 --> 00:28:04.713
distractibility, frankly.

00:28:05.900 --> 00:28:08.410
My guess is that many people with ADHD,

00:28:08.410 --> 00:28:09.900
would not be that hypnotizable,

00:28:09.900 --> 00:28:11.470
but I haven't studied it.

00:28:11.470 --> 00:28:16.470
So, it's possible that for
some people with that disorder,

00:28:17.250 --> 00:28:18.900
training in self-hypnosis might help,

00:28:18.900 --> 00:28:21.140
but we'd have to see how
hypnotizable they were

00:28:21.140 --> 00:28:23.120
and take it from there.

00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:26.130
- I want to return to some of
the underlying neural networks

00:28:26.130 --> 00:28:28.613
and the clinical applications but,

00:28:29.990 --> 00:28:34.890
what sorts of things
aside from the asthma,

00:28:34.890 --> 00:28:39.360
have you used hypnosis successfully for

00:28:39.360 --> 00:28:43.210
or have others used clinical hypnosis for?

00:28:43.210 --> 00:28:46.290
And are there any particular areas of,

00:28:46.290 --> 00:28:51.030
psychiatric challenges or
illnesses, I guess they're called,

00:28:51.030 --> 00:28:56.030
that are particularly amenable
to hypnotic treatment.

00:28:56.400 --> 00:28:57.420
- Yes, there are.

00:28:57.420 --> 00:29:02.420
Hypnosis is very good as a
problem focused treatment.

00:29:03.270 --> 00:29:06.880
It's the oldest western
conception of a psychotherapy,

00:29:06.880 --> 00:29:09.100
and it can be used for specific problems

00:29:09.100 --> 00:29:10.870
in a way that's very helpful.

00:29:10.870 --> 00:29:13.450
We found it very helpful
for stress reduction.

00:29:13.450 --> 00:29:14.550
For helping people deal,

00:29:14.550 --> 00:29:17.220
we're all dealing with stress these days.

00:29:17.220 --> 00:29:20.640
And it's helpful, that mind-body
connection is very helpful

00:29:20.640 --> 00:29:25.560
because part of the problem
with stress is your perception.

00:29:25.560 --> 00:29:27.550
You mentioned earlier
in a sort of good sense,

00:29:27.550 --> 00:29:29.440
you're at a football game or something,

00:29:29.440 --> 00:29:32.060
and you feel the physical reaction.

00:29:32.060 --> 00:29:33.990
That can be a reinforcing thing.

00:29:33.990 --> 00:29:35.860
Wow, this is exciting, let's do it.

00:29:35.860 --> 00:29:37.580
It can also be very distracting.

00:29:37.580 --> 00:29:40.450
So, you're worried about getting COVID

00:29:40.450 --> 00:29:41.713
or you're worried about,

00:29:42.570 --> 00:29:45.220
some other physical problem you have.

00:29:45.220 --> 00:29:48.540
And you notice it in your
body, your body tenses up,

00:29:48.540 --> 00:29:49.600
you start to sweat.

00:29:49.600 --> 00:29:51.640
The sympathetic nervous system goes,

00:29:51.640 --> 00:29:53.160
your heart rate goes up.

00:29:53.160 --> 00:29:55.050
And when you notice that you think,

00:29:55.050 --> 00:29:57.090
oh God, this is really bad.

00:29:57.090 --> 00:29:58.180
And then you feel worse.

00:29:58.180 --> 00:30:00.800
So, it's like a snowball rolling downhill.

00:30:00.800 --> 00:30:03.880
And then you feel worse and
then your body gets worse.

00:30:03.880 --> 00:30:05.500
Hypnosis can be very helpful

00:30:05.500 --> 00:30:07.980
in dissociating somatic reaction

00:30:07.980 --> 00:30:09.300
from psychological reactions.

00:30:09.300 --> 00:30:10.830
So, we teach people

00:30:10.830 --> 00:30:13.100
to imagine their body
floating somewhere safe

00:30:13.100 --> 00:30:14.810
and comfortable like a bath, a lake,

00:30:14.810 --> 00:30:16.740
a hot tub or floating in space,

00:30:16.740 --> 00:30:18.380
and then picture the problem

00:30:18.380 --> 00:30:21.330
that's stressing them
on an imaginary screen,

00:30:21.330 --> 00:30:23.220
with the rule, no matter
what you see on the screen,

00:30:23.220 --> 00:30:24.800
you keep your body comfortable.

00:30:24.800 --> 00:30:27.870
So, at this point, you still
can't control the stress,

00:30:27.870 --> 00:30:30.860
but you can control your
physical reaction to it.

00:30:30.860 --> 00:30:33.050
And that starts you
feeling more in control.

00:30:33.050 --> 00:30:34.600
At least there's one thing I can manage.

00:30:34.600 --> 00:30:36.420
And then you can use it to think through

00:30:36.420 --> 00:30:38.790
or visualize through
one thing you might do

00:30:38.790 --> 00:30:39.720
about that stressor.

00:30:39.720 --> 00:30:42.170
So, hypnosis is very
helpful in controlling

00:30:42.170 --> 00:30:45.473
mind-body interaction
in relation to stress.

00:30:46.420 --> 00:30:49.550
It's very helpful for
people to get to sleep.

00:30:49.550 --> 00:30:51.850
We're having a lot of fun with that.

00:30:51.850 --> 00:30:54.230
I'm getting emails from people who said,

00:30:54.230 --> 00:30:56.040
I haven't slept right in 15 years,

00:30:56.040 --> 00:30:57.403
and now for the first time,

00:30:58.872 --> 00:31:01.520
I'm listening to your app
and I can sleep at night.

00:31:01.520 --> 00:31:03.443
So it's very helpful and again,

00:31:04.312 --> 00:31:06.353
if you wake up in the middle of the night,

00:31:07.460 --> 00:31:09.060
I tell people, don't look at the clock.

00:31:09.060 --> 00:31:10.273
That's an arousal cue.

00:31:11.299 --> 00:31:13.080
You'll wake up more,

00:31:13.080 --> 00:31:16.800
but picture whatever you're
thinking about or worrying about

00:31:16.800 --> 00:31:19.060
on that imaginary screen
while your body is floating.

00:31:19.060 --> 00:31:21.960
So, watch your own movie,
but keep your body floating.

00:31:21.960 --> 00:31:24.650
And many people can use
that to get back to sleep.

00:31:24.650 --> 00:31:29.420
- I've been using self-hypnosis
for sleep for a long time.

00:31:29.420 --> 00:31:30.507
And now the Reveri app,

00:31:30.507 --> 00:31:32.990
and we'll talk about our
relationship to the Reveri app

00:31:32.990 --> 00:31:33.823
and its uses.

00:31:33.823 --> 00:31:36.640
I find it incredibly useful
for falling back asleep

00:31:36.640 --> 00:31:38.610
in the middle of the night.

00:31:38.610 --> 00:31:42.160
And it raises a question,

00:31:42.160 --> 00:31:45.070
I've found and I think I
understand this correctly,

00:31:45.070 --> 00:31:49.940
that one can do self-hypnosis
during the daytime.

00:31:49.940 --> 00:31:52.190
And then if there's an
issue that comes up later,

00:31:52.190 --> 00:31:53.360
like, so for instance,

00:31:53.360 --> 00:31:55.500
do self-hypnosis for stress reduction,

00:31:55.500 --> 00:31:58.170
away from the stressful
event to prepare one

00:31:58.170 --> 00:31:59.510
to deal with stress better.
- Right.

00:31:59.510 --> 00:32:03.880
- Or do hypnosis for
improving the return to sleep.

00:32:03.880 --> 00:32:06.290
And that can be done when you
actually want to go to sleep.

00:32:06.290 --> 00:32:09.043
But it's kind of a training
up of these networks,

00:32:09.043 --> 00:32:10.750
right?
- That's right.

00:32:10.750 --> 00:32:12.770
- So, is there evidence that,

00:32:12.770 --> 00:32:17.430
these brain networks actually
form stronger connections,

00:32:17.430 --> 00:32:19.793
when people do self-hypnosis over time?

00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:23.380
- Well, there's a rule in
neurobiology as you know,

00:32:23.380 --> 00:32:25.550
that neurons that fire
together wire together.

00:32:25.550 --> 00:32:27.000
- Our friend, Carla Shatz.
- Yes, Carla-

00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:28.529
- Not Donald Hebb, by the way.

00:32:28.529 --> 00:32:30.610
[David laughing]
I keep trying to,

00:32:30.610 --> 00:32:32.560
there's a widespread myth in the world

00:32:32.560 --> 00:32:34.930
that is unfortunately,
all over the internet,

00:32:34.930 --> 00:32:37.860
which is that, fire together
wire together was said by,

00:32:37.860 --> 00:32:39.100
this psychologist, Donald Hebb

00:32:39.100 --> 00:32:40.990
Donald Hebb did many important things,

00:32:40.990 --> 00:32:44.390
but it is the neurobiologist Carla Shatz-

00:32:44.390 --> 00:32:45.410
- That's exactly right.
- Who,

00:32:45.410 --> 00:32:48.140
Yes, is at Stanford but was
also at Berkeley and Harvard.

00:32:48.140 --> 00:32:51.250
So, also decent schools.
- That's exactly right.

00:32:51.250 --> 00:32:55.270
- But is at Stanford, who said,
fire together wire together.

00:32:55.270 --> 00:32:56.690
And so she deserves,
- Right.

00:32:56.690 --> 00:32:58.520
- the credit for that statement.

00:32:58.520 --> 00:33:02.143
Yeah, so with repeated
use of self-hypnosis,

00:33:03.210 --> 00:33:06.530
one could imagine that these
networks are getting stronger.

00:33:06.530 --> 00:33:07.720
- I would think so.

00:33:07.720 --> 00:33:10.080
We don't have evidence of that yet,

00:33:10.080 --> 00:33:14.703
but long term potentiation
provides a pathway

00:33:16.230 --> 00:33:18.860
and you've described them on
your program, a number of times

00:33:18.860 --> 00:33:22.050
that allow for repeated
activation of a network

00:33:22.050 --> 00:33:25.300
to actually build new
connections that work.

00:33:25.300 --> 00:33:26.480
And at the least,

00:33:26.480 --> 00:33:28.780
even from a learning and
memory point of view,

00:33:30.200 --> 00:33:32.650
memory is all a network of associations.

00:33:32.650 --> 00:33:34.370
That's how we remember things.

00:33:34.370 --> 00:33:37.050
And the example I'd like to give is,

00:33:37.050 --> 00:33:39.250
you go back to your grade school

00:33:39.250 --> 00:33:41.510
and you see these little tiny lockers

00:33:41.510 --> 00:33:43.500
and the size is all wrong.

00:33:43.500 --> 00:33:45.280
And you suddenly have a flood of memories

00:33:45.280 --> 00:33:47.530
that were obviously stored there,

00:33:47.530 --> 00:33:48.540
but you just didn't think of.

00:33:48.540 --> 00:33:52.370
So, context and association
is what memory's about.

00:33:52.370 --> 00:33:55.350
If you start to acquire
memories about a problem.

00:33:55.350 --> 00:33:57.340
So, one thing we use hypnosis for

00:33:57.340 --> 00:33:59.160
is treating phobias, for example.

00:33:59.160 --> 00:34:01.100
And the problem with
people who have phobias,

00:34:01.100 --> 00:34:02.440
like airplane phobias

00:34:02.440 --> 00:34:06.370
or crossing a bridge or being up high,

00:34:06.370 --> 00:34:08.660
is that the more they avoid it the more,

00:34:08.660 --> 00:34:12.930
the only source of associations
and memories is their fear.

00:34:12.930 --> 00:34:14.840
They don't have any
good experiences with it

00:34:14.840 --> 00:34:16.110
'cause they avoid it.

00:34:16.110 --> 00:34:17.400
It's like get back on the horse

00:34:17.400 --> 00:34:19.270
after you fall off kind of thing.

00:34:19.270 --> 00:34:21.530
And with hypnosis,

00:34:21.530 --> 00:34:26.400
if you can start people able
to manage their anxiety enough

00:34:26.400 --> 00:34:29.800
that they can have more, a
wider array of experiences,

00:34:29.800 --> 00:34:32.420
they start to have a
network of associations

00:34:32.420 --> 00:34:34.860
that isn't so negative
and may even be positive.

00:34:34.860 --> 00:34:36.900
- So it's almost like
a, sorry to interrupt,

00:34:36.900 --> 00:34:37.733
but I have to ask.

00:34:37.733 --> 00:34:39.710
- Sure
- It's almost like a,

00:34:39.710 --> 00:34:43.078
exposure therapy done in the mind.

00:34:43.078 --> 00:34:44.330
- Yes.
- It's always in the mind.

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:46.880
I mean, even that exposure
to, if I have a snake phobia,

00:34:46.880 --> 00:34:48.870
which I don't, I don't like snakes,

00:34:48.870 --> 00:34:50.807
but I don't think it qualifies
as a full blown phobia.

00:34:50.807 --> 00:34:52.870
I think I have a healthy fear of snakes.

00:34:52.870 --> 00:34:54.593
- Yeah.
- But if,

00:34:55.470 --> 00:34:57.750
let's say I had a snake phobia,

00:34:57.750 --> 00:35:01.730
the typical approach in, would be,

00:35:01.730 --> 00:35:03.230
cognitive behavioral approaches, right,

00:35:03.230 --> 00:35:05.060
would be to show a picture of a snake,

00:35:05.060 --> 00:35:06.470
then a rubber snake, then a real snake.

00:35:06.470 --> 00:35:07.990
Eventually the person is,
- Yeah.

00:35:07.990 --> 00:35:10.360
- holding a boa constrictor
or something like that.

00:35:10.360 --> 00:35:11.193
- [David] Right.

00:35:11.193 --> 00:35:12.620
- That's all in the mind

00:35:12.620 --> 00:35:14.840
because it's all translate
into nervous system signals.

00:35:14.840 --> 00:35:16.230
But with hypnosis,

00:35:16.230 --> 00:35:19.400
sounds like you can give a
number of positive experiences

00:35:19.400 --> 00:35:21.600
without having to use any props,

00:35:21.600 --> 00:35:23.730
without having bring any
animals into the room.

00:35:23.730 --> 00:35:24.563
- Right, I-

00:35:24.563 --> 00:35:26.040
- Drive someone across
the bridge, is that right?

00:35:26.040 --> 00:35:27.650
- Yes, I had a woman,

00:35:27.650 --> 00:35:29.870
who was a very successful business woman,

00:35:29.870 --> 00:35:32.030
high level in a corporation.

00:35:32.030 --> 00:35:33.680
I had a terrible dog phobia.

00:35:33.680 --> 00:35:37.660
And so I had her imagine that
somebody brought in the dog

00:35:37.660 --> 00:35:39.207
to the room and I said,
"what are you doing?"

00:35:39.207 --> 00:35:41.410
And you could see her getting tense.

00:35:41.410 --> 00:35:44.430
And she said, "Well, I'm waiting
to see what the dog does."

00:35:44.430 --> 00:35:46.650
And I said, "If somebody
who works for you,

00:35:46.650 --> 00:35:47.730
comes into your office,

00:35:47.730 --> 00:35:50.570
would you freeze and wait
to see what they did?"

00:35:50.570 --> 00:35:51.920
And she said, "Of course not,

00:35:51.920 --> 00:35:53.053
I tell 'em what to do."

00:35:53.053 --> 00:35:56.700
[laughs] And I said, "Well, so
you're immobilizing yourself,

00:35:56.700 --> 00:35:59.100
the power isn't with
the dog it's with you,

00:35:59.100 --> 00:36:01.830
so, imagine what you
might do to engage the dog

00:36:01.830 --> 00:36:04.500
and help control the situation."

00:36:04.500 --> 00:36:05.500
And she said, "Thanks."

00:36:05.500 --> 00:36:06.991
And this reminds me of,

00:36:06.991 --> 00:36:10.050
one of my favorite stories
about hypnosis that,

00:36:10.050 --> 00:36:13.360
my father was seeing a woman
who lived in Midtown Manhattan

00:36:13.360 --> 00:36:15.090
and had a horrible dog phobia.

00:36:15.090 --> 00:36:18.570
She'd dropped things, she'd
spill coffee if she saw a dog.

00:36:18.570 --> 00:36:20.640
She would time her trips to the store,

00:36:20.640 --> 00:36:22.070
when she thought it was least likely

00:36:22.070 --> 00:36:23.350
that people would be walking dogs.

00:36:23.350 --> 00:36:25.202
- Now that wouldn't be
possible, everyone in-

00:36:25.202 --> 00:36:26.035
- [laughs] Everyone in New York has a dog

00:36:26.035 --> 00:36:28.250
- It's like a fleet of French Bulldogs,

00:36:28.250 --> 00:36:30.930
- Right, exactly.
- taking over New York City.

00:36:30.930 --> 00:36:35.160
- So, he taught her to
think of dog as a friend,

00:36:35.160 --> 00:36:37.990
have a neighbor who had a dog,

00:36:37.990 --> 00:36:40.420
bring the dog over but
hold the dog by the collar

00:36:40.420 --> 00:36:41.540
and make sure,

00:36:41.540 --> 00:36:44.610
and gradually she was able
to stroke the dog and say,

00:36:44.610 --> 00:36:47.240
dog friend and distinguish
between wild and tame animals.

00:36:47.240 --> 00:36:49.410
There are animals, you should
be afraid of [murmurs].

00:36:49.410 --> 00:36:50.650
So, she seemed to be doing better.

00:36:50.650 --> 00:36:53.180
He called back about three months later

00:36:53.180 --> 00:36:54.563
and asked for her.

00:36:55.637 --> 00:36:57.090
"Well, who's calling?" the son said.

00:36:57.090 --> 00:36:58.890
And he said, "Dr. Spiegel."

00:36:58.890 --> 00:37:01.150
And the boy said, "That's weird."

00:37:01.150 --> 00:37:02.520
And my father said, "What's weird?"

00:37:02.520 --> 00:37:05.300
He said, "Spiegel's in heat."

00:37:05.300 --> 00:37:07.830
She had bought a dog,
- I love it.

00:37:07.830 --> 00:37:08.828
- and named it, Spiegel.

00:37:08.828 --> 00:37:09.940
- I love it.
- Talk about transference.

00:37:09.940 --> 00:37:11.290
- I love it.
[David laughing]

00:37:11.290 --> 00:37:13.100
But it really speaks to the power of this.

00:37:13.100 --> 00:37:16.723
And it brings me back to this issue.

00:37:18.280 --> 00:37:21.400
So, what is different about,

00:37:21.400 --> 00:37:25.690
what your father did in
that case with this woman,

00:37:25.690 --> 00:37:29.200
in terms of what happened in hypnosis

00:37:29.200 --> 00:37:30.240
that allowed her to go

00:37:30.240 --> 00:37:32.570
from being completely terrified of dogs

00:37:32.570 --> 00:37:35.820
to owning a dog and naming
it after your father?

00:37:35.820 --> 00:37:38.070
Which I find amusing.
- Yeah.

00:37:38.070 --> 00:37:39.430
- But that's different

00:37:39.430 --> 00:37:41.270
than just the two of them sitting down

00:37:41.270 --> 00:37:43.210
and talking about it, right?

00:37:43.210 --> 00:37:44.960
You know, in therapy,

00:37:44.960 --> 00:37:47.520
narrative is a huge component.
- Right.

00:37:47.520 --> 00:37:49.930
- And in hypnosis narrative
is a huge component.

00:37:49.930 --> 00:37:52.370
- Right.
- So it must be that,

00:37:52.370 --> 00:37:54.650
the brain state is what
is really different.

00:37:54.650 --> 00:37:57.420
Because we'll talk about
trauma in a few minutes.

00:37:57.420 --> 00:38:01.710
But I think people who
have trauma or phobias,

00:38:01.710 --> 00:38:06.190
certainly could have a
conversation about it.

00:38:06.190 --> 00:38:07.410
Some of them might freeze up.

00:38:07.410 --> 00:38:10.010
Some of them might lose their
articulation and so forth.

00:38:10.010 --> 00:38:13.200
But what is different about that state

00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:15.260
that combines with narrative,

00:38:15.260 --> 00:38:18.720
you think to allow these
underlying neural networks

00:38:18.720 --> 00:38:20.120
to engage her to change?

00:38:20.120 --> 00:38:22.960
'Cause I find this so
fascinating because we're a-

00:38:22.960 --> 00:38:26.700
Every attempt at dealing
with stress or phobia

00:38:26.700 --> 00:38:28.980
in the clinical setting
involves some discussion

00:38:28.980 --> 00:38:30.400
about what it is.
- Yes.

00:38:30.400 --> 00:38:32.410
- But, here we're not talking about

00:38:32.410 --> 00:38:33.840
any medication being introduced,

00:38:33.840 --> 00:38:35.710
at least not in these
particular circumstances.

00:38:35.710 --> 00:38:37.430
- Right.
- So,

00:38:37.430 --> 00:38:39.920
I realize it's kind of an obvious question

00:38:39.920 --> 00:38:42.650
like, it has to be some
difference in brain activity,

00:38:42.650 --> 00:38:44.840
but I find that to be incredible.

00:38:44.840 --> 00:38:48.170
The control variable
there is the brain state.

00:38:48.170 --> 00:38:50.543
It's not, what's spoken.

00:38:51.410 --> 00:38:54.533
- You're raising a couple of
very important issues, Andrew.

00:38:56.420 --> 00:38:59.190
We talked earlier about with
systematic desensitization,

00:38:59.190 --> 00:39:02.720
where you sort of lay
out a hierarchy of things

00:39:02.720 --> 00:39:04.250
and do it one at a time.

00:39:04.250 --> 00:39:07.440
I think of this as
unsystematic desensitization,

00:39:07.440 --> 00:39:09.970
because you're changing mental states.

00:39:09.970 --> 00:39:12.420
And I think there's more and more evidence

00:39:12.420 --> 00:39:15.380
that mental state change itself
has therapeutic potential.

00:39:15.380 --> 00:39:17.990
We're seeing that with
ketamine treating depression,

00:39:17.990 --> 00:39:19.463
the dissociogenic drug.

00:39:21.360 --> 00:39:23.470
We know it every morning when we wake up,

00:39:23.470 --> 00:39:24.390
that problem, when you know,

00:39:24.390 --> 00:39:27.360
you made the mistake of reading
a nasty email at 11:00 PM.

00:39:27.360 --> 00:39:28.450
You didn't know what to do.

00:39:28.450 --> 00:39:29.390
You wake up in the morning thinking,

00:39:29.390 --> 00:39:31.770
oh, that idiot, yeah,
here's what I'm going to do.

00:39:31.770 --> 00:39:34.140
So, just changing mental state,

00:39:34.140 --> 00:39:36.260
itself has therapeutic potential.

00:39:36.260 --> 00:39:39.800
And I think we underestimate
our ability to regulate

00:39:39.800 --> 00:39:42.783
and change responses to be cognitively,

00:39:43.630 --> 00:39:46.490
emotionally and somatically flexible.

00:39:46.490 --> 00:39:48.540
And so, we do things, you're right,

00:39:48.540 --> 00:39:51.460
that follow similar principles
of facing a problem,

00:39:51.460 --> 00:39:53.470
seeing it from a different point of view.

00:39:53.470 --> 00:39:54.890
And you've done a really,

00:39:54.890 --> 00:39:56.970
a nice podcast on trauma and stress

00:39:56.970 --> 00:39:59.930
and how you have to expose yourself to it,

00:39:59.930 --> 00:40:02.370
not avoid it, as we talked about before.

00:40:02.370 --> 00:40:06.280
And then find some way to reconnect to it,

00:40:06.280 --> 00:40:08.690
to substitute something
that can make you feel good,

00:40:08.690 --> 00:40:10.500
rather than bad,

00:40:10.500 --> 00:40:13.040
so that you activate
other centers of the brain

00:40:13.040 --> 00:40:15.150
like mesolimbic reward system.

00:40:15.150 --> 00:40:19.990
And so, I that with hypnosis
and you can do it much faster.

00:40:19.990 --> 00:40:21.940
People don't think they can but they can.

00:40:21.940 --> 00:40:24.710
If you are having, right now
that physical experience,

00:40:24.710 --> 00:40:25.543
I'm thinking about this

00:40:25.543 --> 00:40:29.010
but I'm not feeling as bad as I used to.

00:40:29.010 --> 00:40:30.570
That can be a powerful thing

00:40:30.570 --> 00:40:32.100
and you can do it with hypnosis.

00:40:32.100 --> 00:40:34.910
So, a woman came to see me,

00:40:34.910 --> 00:40:36.900
who had suffered an attempted rape.

00:40:36.900 --> 00:40:38.500
It was getting dark,

00:40:38.500 --> 00:40:40.240
she was coming back from the grocery store

00:40:40.240 --> 00:40:41.810
and this guy grabs her

00:40:41.810 --> 00:40:43.700
and wants to get her
up into her apartment.

00:40:43.700 --> 00:40:44.740
It's outside her apartment.

00:40:44.740 --> 00:40:47.130
And she starts fighting with him

00:40:47.130 --> 00:40:49.720
and she winds up with a
basilar skull fracture.

00:40:49.720 --> 00:40:51.340
He runs away.

00:40:51.340 --> 00:40:52.670
The cops come,

00:40:52.670 --> 00:40:54.170
since she hadn't been raped, they left,

00:40:54.170 --> 00:40:55.003
they weren't interested.

00:40:55.003 --> 00:40:56.980
And she wanted to use hypnosis

00:40:56.980 --> 00:40:59.440
to get a better image of
what this guy looked like,

00:40:59.440 --> 00:41:01.190
which is a painful, upsetting thing.

00:41:01.190 --> 00:41:03.500
So, she was quite hypnotizable.

00:41:03.500 --> 00:41:04.710
I got her floating.

00:41:04.710 --> 00:41:06.140
I say, you're safe and comfortable, now,

00:41:06.140 --> 00:41:08.460
nothing can happen that
will harm your body.

00:41:08.460 --> 00:41:10.370
But on the left side of the screen,

00:41:10.370 --> 00:41:12.470
I want you to picture this guy

00:41:12.470 --> 00:41:14.620
and his approaching and what's happening.

00:41:14.620 --> 00:41:16.660
And she said, I really, the light,

00:41:16.660 --> 00:41:17.493
it was getting dark.

00:41:17.493 --> 00:41:20.260
I really can't see much
of his facial features,

00:41:20.260 --> 00:41:21.800
but I do recognize something,

00:41:21.800 --> 00:41:24.030
I hadn't allowed myself to remember.

00:41:24.030 --> 00:41:25.370
If he gets me upstairs,

00:41:25.370 --> 00:41:28.200
he doesn't just want to rape
me, he's going to kill me.

00:41:28.200 --> 00:41:31.850
And so, in some ways, what
she was seeing was even worse.

00:41:31.850 --> 00:41:34.200
So, you're thinking, good, Spiegel,

00:41:34.200 --> 00:41:37.030
you made her even more
frightened than she was before.

00:41:37.030 --> 00:41:40.630
But as you had pointed out
in your PTSD stress lecture,

00:41:40.630 --> 00:41:42.270
you've got to confront the trauma,

00:41:42.270 --> 00:41:45.500
to restructure your understanding of it.

00:41:45.500 --> 00:41:47.140
So, on the other side of the screen,

00:41:47.140 --> 00:41:48.403
I had her picture,

00:41:50.749 --> 00:41:53.080
what are you doing to protect yourself?

00:41:53.080 --> 00:41:56.250
And everybody in a trauma
situation engages in some strategy

00:41:56.250 --> 00:41:57.590
of self protection.

00:41:57.590 --> 00:41:59.460
That's the salience network kicking in.

00:41:59.460 --> 00:42:02.600
And she said, "You know what?"

00:42:02.600 --> 00:42:05.040
He's surprised that
I'm fighting that hard.

00:42:05.040 --> 00:42:06.770
He didn't think I would.

00:42:06.770 --> 00:42:08.680
And so, she realized on the one hand

00:42:08.680 --> 00:42:10.580
that it was even worse
than she thought it was.

00:42:10.580 --> 00:42:11.490
But on the other hand

00:42:11.490 --> 00:42:13.800
that she actually probably saved her life.

00:42:13.800 --> 00:42:16.180
And so, it was a way of,

00:42:16.180 --> 00:42:18.830
helping her restructure her
experience of the trauma

00:42:18.830 --> 00:42:20.670
and make it more tolerable.

00:42:20.670 --> 00:42:22.290
So that helped with her-

00:42:22.290 --> 00:42:23.195
She didn't recog-

00:42:23.195 --> 00:42:25.220
She couldn't identify the guy,

00:42:25.220 --> 00:42:29.090
but it helped her restructure
and understand her experience.

00:42:29.090 --> 00:42:31.110
And that's something that you can do

00:42:31.110 --> 00:42:33.890
in just talking, straight
out psychotherapy.

00:42:33.890 --> 00:42:36.650
But sometimes you can do
it a hell of a lot faster

00:42:36.650 --> 00:42:39.210
and more efficiently using hypnosis.

00:42:39.210 --> 00:42:41.690
And there is one randomized
trial out of Israel

00:42:41.690 --> 00:42:44.170
that shows that adding
hypnosis to PTSD treatment,

00:42:44.170 --> 00:42:46.150
actually improves outcome.

00:42:46.150 --> 00:42:51.150
So it's a way of accomplishing
things that we understand

00:42:52.320 --> 00:42:56.160
in the broader psychotherapy
world, but much more quickly

00:42:56.160 --> 00:42:58.380
and sometimes effectively.

00:42:58.380 --> 00:43:01.610
- Yeah, it sounds like going
somewhat into the state

00:43:01.610 --> 00:43:03.160
that one is trying to deal with,

00:43:03.160 --> 00:43:05.610
but then dissociating
from that state is key.

00:43:05.610 --> 00:43:07.020
And I could imagine,

00:43:07.020 --> 00:43:09.410
and I've been open about
this on various podcasts.

00:43:09.410 --> 00:43:13.323
I've done a lot of an
analysis over the years.

00:43:14.544 --> 00:43:17.460
I've experienced myself
that in those sessions,

00:43:17.460 --> 00:43:19.180
depending on how I show up to them,

00:43:19.180 --> 00:43:22.310
I might just get kind of a
laundry list of what happened

00:43:22.310 --> 00:43:24.110
as opposed to actually feeling anything,

00:43:24.110 --> 00:43:26.310
around what happened.
- Right.

00:43:26.310 --> 00:43:28.270
- And I think people
probably vary in the extent

00:43:28.270 --> 00:43:30.770
to which they can drop into feeling states

00:43:30.770 --> 00:43:33.250
and it can depend on the day.

00:43:33.250 --> 00:43:36.070
It can depend on how well
you slept the night before

00:43:36.070 --> 00:43:36.960
and so on.

00:43:36.960 --> 00:43:38.320
- There's one thing I might add, Andrew,

00:43:38.320 --> 00:43:40.020
- Yes.
- And that is,

00:43:40.020 --> 00:43:41.940
there's a notion to late Gordon Bower,

00:43:41.940 --> 00:43:45.240
we just had a memorial
for Gordon at Stanford.

00:43:45.240 --> 00:43:47.120
He died about a year ago.

00:43:47.120 --> 00:43:49.220
Brilliant cognitive psychologist,

00:43:49.220 --> 00:43:52.230
one of the founders of
cognitive psychology at Stanford

00:43:52.230 --> 00:43:53.330
and a great pitcher.

00:43:53.330 --> 00:43:55.400
He almost became a Major League pitcher

00:43:55.400 --> 00:43:57.420
but he decided to go
to grad school instead,

00:43:57.420 --> 00:43:58.763
and I'm glad he did.

00:43:59.930 --> 00:44:03.170
But Gordon helped establish the concept of

00:44:03.170 --> 00:44:04.430
state dependent memory

00:44:04.430 --> 00:44:07.140
that when you're in a
certain mental state,

00:44:07.140 --> 00:44:09.210
you enhance your ability to
remember things about it.

00:44:09.210 --> 00:44:11.450
And sort of the bad example of that is

00:44:11.450 --> 00:44:12.800
the drunk who hides the bottle

00:44:12.800 --> 00:44:14.140
and can't remember where he put it

00:44:14.140 --> 00:44:15.050
until he gets drunk again

00:44:15.050 --> 00:44:18.220
and he's in that same mental state.

00:44:18.220 --> 00:44:22.490
People go into dissociative
states when they're traumatized.

00:44:22.490 --> 00:44:26.090
So in a way, hypnosis
is helping them remember

00:44:26.090 --> 00:44:27.380
and deal with the memories better

00:44:27.380 --> 00:44:29.400
because they're more in the mental state

00:44:29.400 --> 00:44:30.570
that is more like what happened.

00:44:30.570 --> 00:44:32.880
And most rape victims will tell you,

00:44:32.880 --> 00:44:34.640
I was floating above my body,

00:44:34.640 --> 00:44:37.323
feeling sorry for the woman
being assaulted below.

00:44:39.684 --> 00:44:42.017
People in traumatic
episodes, they just say,

00:44:42.017 --> 00:44:43.960
you know, I blank out, I
don't know what's happening,

00:44:43.960 --> 00:44:44.970
I'm on autopilot

00:44:44.970 --> 00:44:47.340
and that's a kind of self hypnotic state.

00:44:47.340 --> 00:44:50.510
So, when you use hypnosis
to help them deal

00:44:50.510 --> 00:44:51.960
with a traumatic memory,

00:44:51.960 --> 00:44:53.510
you're making the state they're in,

00:44:53.510 --> 00:44:55.600
right there in your office with you,

00:44:55.600 --> 00:44:58.060
more congruent to the
state they were likely in,

00:44:58.060 --> 00:44:59.200
when the trauma happened.

00:44:59.200 --> 00:45:02.640
And I think that is part of
what helps facilitate treatment

00:45:02.640 --> 00:45:04.600
of trauma related disorders.

00:45:04.600 --> 00:45:05.860
- I see.

00:45:05.860 --> 00:45:09.360
So that makes me have to ask,
every question I have to ask,

00:45:09.360 --> 00:45:11.860
'cause I really feel it as
a almost like compulsion,

00:45:11.860 --> 00:45:16.830
then if dissociation during
a traumatic episode is,

00:45:16.830 --> 00:45:19.230
it's a part of the adaptive strategy.

00:45:19.230 --> 00:45:20.070
- Right.

00:45:20.070 --> 00:45:23.070
- But it creates certain issues,
it creates problems, right?

00:45:24.560 --> 00:45:27.260
Why would something like ketamine,

00:45:27.260 --> 00:45:29.840
which creates a dissociative state,

00:45:29.840 --> 00:45:32.340
be useful for the treatment of trauma?

00:45:32.340 --> 00:45:34.610
This is what I'm confused about these days

00:45:34.610 --> 00:45:36.780
because our colleague, Karl Deisseroth,

00:45:36.780 --> 00:45:38.670
who's also been on this podcast

00:45:38.670 --> 00:45:40.510
and his coworkers have figured out,

00:45:40.510 --> 00:45:43.780
okay, there's these layer one
networks in the neocortex,

00:45:43.780 --> 00:45:45.390
and those are involved
in dissociative state.

00:45:45.390 --> 00:45:46.710
And so we're starting to
gain some understanding

00:45:46.710 --> 00:45:48.877
of how ketamine works at a neural level.

00:45:48.877 --> 00:45:51.200
- Right.
- It does seem,

00:45:51.200 --> 00:45:54.890
as if for certain populations
it can be a useful treatment.

00:45:54.890 --> 00:45:55.930
I don't know, I've never tried it.

00:45:55.930 --> 00:45:57.460
I don't know what the
current status of that is,

00:45:57.460 --> 00:45:58.780
but it is legal.

00:45:58.780 --> 00:46:00.000
It is allowed at least in,

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:03.310
it's FDA approved and it's in use.

00:46:03.310 --> 00:46:06.880
Why would dissociative states be useful

00:46:06.880 --> 00:46:09.200
if some element of dissociation

00:46:09.200 --> 00:46:13.622
is what gave rise to the trauma
memory in the first place?

00:46:13.622 --> 00:46:16.230
- Well, yeah, and Karl
had a brilliant paper

00:46:16.230 --> 00:46:17.430
in nature where he,

00:46:17.430 --> 00:46:20.240
it was from rats to humans in one paper.

00:46:20.240 --> 00:46:22.870
And he showed that there's
this rhythmic discharge

00:46:22.870 --> 00:46:24.393
in the retrosplenial region,

00:46:26.300 --> 00:46:28.080
that is triggered by ketamine.

00:46:28.080 --> 00:46:32.000
And the rats actually showed
dissociative like behavior,

00:46:32.000 --> 00:46:35.330
in that they would a hot pad
that they ordinarily wouldn't

00:46:35.330 --> 00:46:37.400
and they didn't seem to
have much pain in their paw.

00:46:37.400 --> 00:46:42.400
And he then had a male subject
who had implanted electrodes-

00:46:43.182 --> 00:46:45.600
- Human, yeah.
- A human subject, yeah.

00:46:45.600 --> 00:46:50.600
And the electrodes had picked
up this rhythmic activity.

00:46:50.720 --> 00:46:51.860
And when they did,

00:46:51.860 --> 00:46:54.100
he would report being
in a dissociative state.

00:46:54.100 --> 00:46:55.760
And his description was,

00:46:55.760 --> 00:46:57.770
it's like being a pilot of an airplane.

00:46:57.770 --> 00:47:00.560
And then I felt myself
walking out of the cockpit

00:47:00.560 --> 00:47:01.970
and the plane was still flying and-

00:47:01.970 --> 00:47:03.953
- It sounds terrifying to me.
[David laughing]

00:47:03.953 --> 00:47:04.786
It sounds terrify terrifying.
- That's the thing-

00:47:04.786 --> 00:47:06.540
- I want to be in my body,
[David laughing]

00:47:06.540 --> 00:47:08.730
most of the time, you know.

00:47:08.730 --> 00:47:09.770
- That's right but,

00:47:09.770 --> 00:47:13.050
the point is, in a way
the principal, Andrew,

00:47:13.050 --> 00:47:14.113
is like, the principle you said,

00:47:14.113 --> 00:47:17.900
that you need to re-confront
a traumatic situation

00:47:17.900 --> 00:47:21.290
before you can modulate
your associations to it.

00:47:21.290 --> 00:47:23.773
So, you have to accept
it, accept the arousal,

00:47:24.610 --> 00:47:25.860
put some boundaries around it

00:47:25.860 --> 00:47:28.310
and then figure out how you
can approach that problem

00:47:28.310 --> 00:47:29.830
or how you did approach that problem

00:47:29.830 --> 00:47:31.310
from a different point of view.

00:47:31.310 --> 00:47:33.000
So, it does not surprise-

00:47:33.000 --> 00:47:34.860
In fact, we've studied,

00:47:34.860 --> 00:47:38.100
people who disassociated during
the Loma Prieta earthquake

00:47:38.100 --> 00:47:40.410
and the Oakland-Berkeley firestorm.

00:47:40.410 --> 00:47:41.960
- I remember both those well.
- Yeah.

00:47:41.960 --> 00:47:42.940
- Earthquakes follow me.

00:47:42.940 --> 00:47:45.030
Then I move south and
then the Northridge quake-

00:47:45.030 --> 00:47:45.863
- I'm going to keep away from

00:47:45.863 --> 00:47:46.779
So, there'll be one later, this afternoon.

00:47:46.779 --> 00:47:48.410
[David laughing]

00:47:48.410 --> 00:47:50.390
- I'm starting to dissociating.

00:47:50.390 --> 00:47:55.330
So, dissociation does
compartmentalize experience,

00:47:55.330 --> 00:47:58.110
but that means from the point
of view of treating trauma,

00:47:58.110 --> 00:48:00.390
it's an inhibition.

00:48:00.390 --> 00:48:01.520
You don't engage it.

00:48:01.520 --> 00:48:03.110
It's like it happened over there.

00:48:03.110 --> 00:48:05.740
And I think what happens is that people,

00:48:05.740 --> 00:48:07.650
are sometimes too good at being able

00:48:07.650 --> 00:48:09.680
to separate themselves
from their recollection.

00:48:09.680 --> 00:48:11.550
So it's in there somewhere.

00:48:11.550 --> 00:48:13.320
It's out of sight but
it's not out of mind.

00:48:13.320 --> 00:48:15.650
It's having effects on you,
but you can't deal with it.

00:48:15.650 --> 00:48:17.310
You can't reprocess it.

00:48:17.310 --> 00:48:20.830
So I do think one reason
ketamine might work

00:48:20.830 --> 00:48:25.520
is that in fact, it allows you to keep,

00:48:25.520 --> 00:48:29.050
to re-approach the dissociative experience

00:48:29.050 --> 00:48:31.020
in a way that you can
then start to think about

00:48:31.020 --> 00:48:32.220
and do something about it.

00:48:32.220 --> 00:48:34.340
And just the fact you
can turn it on and off.

00:48:34.340 --> 00:48:37.540
And that's also where
self-hypnosis is so helpful.

00:48:37.540 --> 00:48:39.350
It's not something that
just comes over you

00:48:39.350 --> 00:48:40.183
and happens to you.

00:48:40.183 --> 00:48:41.580
It's something you can make happen.

00:48:41.580 --> 00:48:43.860
You can control it, you
can do something with it.

00:48:43.860 --> 00:48:46.530
So, you feel less helpless
and out of control.

00:48:46.530 --> 00:48:48.410
The essence of trauma is helplessness.

00:48:48.410 --> 00:48:50.100
It's not fear, it's not pain.

00:48:50.100 --> 00:48:50.970
It's helplessness.

00:48:50.970 --> 00:48:52.620
You become an object.

00:48:52.620 --> 00:48:54.170
You become just your body.

00:48:54.170 --> 00:48:55.287
You don't control what's going on.

00:48:55.287 --> 00:48:57.240
And we're not used to that.

00:48:57.240 --> 00:49:00.370
You and I have discussed
this breathing paper

00:49:00.370 --> 00:49:02.520
on anticipation of breathing.

00:49:02.520 --> 00:49:04.300
And it's not whether you breathe,

00:49:04.300 --> 00:49:06.810
inhale or exhale or hold your breath,

00:49:06.810 --> 00:49:10.290
it's that if you think you
can inhale and you can't,

00:49:10.290 --> 00:49:12.680
that is really upsetting, understandably.

00:49:12.680 --> 00:49:15.070
And so, the issue is control

00:49:15.070 --> 00:49:18.100
and hypnosis, which has
this terrible reputation

00:49:18.100 --> 00:49:21.880
of taking away control
is actually a superb way

00:49:21.880 --> 00:49:24.503
of enhancing your control
over mind and body.

00:49:25.750 --> 00:49:29.100
- I love that and it reminds
me that naming is so important.

00:49:29.100 --> 00:49:31.590
You almost wonder if self-hypnosis

00:49:31.590 --> 00:49:34.610
and clinical hypnosis had
been called something else,

00:49:34.610 --> 00:49:38.260
that it would've been separated
out from stage hypnosis

00:49:38.260 --> 00:49:42.780
in a way that would make it less scary,

00:49:42.780 --> 00:49:46.710
weird, complicated for people to embrace.

00:49:46.710 --> 00:49:47.950
But,
- Yeah.

00:49:47.950 --> 00:49:50.760
- part of the reason for
having this discussion is,

00:49:50.760 --> 00:49:51.983
I've had great experiences with hypnosis,

00:49:51.983 --> 00:49:54.230
that I've seen the data,

00:49:54.230 --> 00:49:55.740
we're talking about a
lot of clinical examples.

00:49:55.740 --> 00:49:57.270
It's incredibly powerful

00:49:57.270 --> 00:50:00.740
and it boils right down
to neural brain states.

00:50:00.740 --> 00:50:02.540
- Right.
- And,

00:50:02.540 --> 00:50:03.790
I think in the years to come,

00:50:03.790 --> 00:50:08.310
it's going to become more
widespread along those lines.

00:50:08.310 --> 00:50:09.310
How quickly,

00:50:09.310 --> 00:50:11.700
you've described some examples

00:50:11.700 --> 00:50:14.790
of people getting relief very quickly.

00:50:14.790 --> 00:50:16.270
- Right.
- How permanent,

00:50:16.270 --> 00:50:17.543
are those changes?

00:50:18.380 --> 00:50:21.570
Is there a need for follow
up and related to that,

00:50:21.570 --> 00:50:23.180
I'm sure a number of people
are listening to this

00:50:23.180 --> 00:50:26.230
and thinking, wonderful,
I'd love to get hypnotized

00:50:26.230 --> 00:50:29.680
for any number of different
things by Dr. Spiegel

00:50:29.680 --> 00:50:32.380
or somebody else expert
in clinical hypnosis,

00:50:32.380 --> 00:50:35.150
but they might not have access to you

00:50:35.150 --> 00:50:37.430
or somebody with similar training.

00:50:37.430 --> 00:50:39.240
So what is the power?

00:50:39.240 --> 00:50:40.950
So, how quickly does it work?

00:50:40.950 --> 00:50:42.720
How long lasting are those changes?

00:50:42.720 --> 00:50:44.490
And then,

00:50:44.490 --> 00:50:46.970
is it necessary to work
with a clinical hypnotist?

00:50:46.970 --> 00:50:48.860
And is it better to do that

00:50:48.860 --> 00:50:50.690
than self-hypnosis and so on and so forth?

00:50:50.690 --> 00:50:52.366
Maybe you could just give us a contour

00:50:52.366 --> 00:50:56.230
of the landscape of directed
and self-directed treatment.

00:50:56.230 --> 00:50:58.363
- Well, typically,

00:50:59.850 --> 00:51:03.240
most people start by coming
to see a clinician like me.

00:51:03.240 --> 00:51:05.960
It's better to see
someone who has licensing

00:51:05.960 --> 00:51:08.690
and training in their
professional discipline,

00:51:08.690 --> 00:51:11.912
medicine, psychology, dentistry, whatever.

00:51:11.912 --> 00:51:13.259
- 'Cause there are a lot
of hypnotists out there,

00:51:13.259 --> 00:51:14.254
who are just hypnotist.
- Right.

00:51:14.254 --> 00:51:15.847
Just hypnotists.
- Oh, okay.

00:51:15.847 --> 00:51:17.780
- And the key issue is,

00:51:17.780 --> 00:51:19.770
somebody who can really
assess what your problem is

00:51:19.770 --> 00:51:21.050
and make sure that you're not,

00:51:21.050 --> 00:51:22.970
talking someone into
reducing their chest pain,

00:51:22.970 --> 00:51:26.230
rather than getting their
coronary artery problem-

00:51:26.230 --> 00:51:27.590
- 'Cause they could
have a real issue there.

00:51:27.590 --> 00:51:30.670
- They could, right.
- That hypnosis might adjust

00:51:30.670 --> 00:51:33.170
but wouldn't deal with the
deeper underlying issue.

00:51:33.170 --> 00:51:34.003
- That's right.

00:51:34.003 --> 00:51:35.280
On the other hand.

00:51:35.280 --> 00:51:37.090
And typically when I use it with people,

00:51:37.090 --> 00:51:39.720
I often only see them once
or twice or periodically,

00:51:39.720 --> 00:51:41.060
but not every week.

00:51:41.060 --> 00:51:42.290
And certainly not every day,

00:51:42.290 --> 00:51:43.137
if they have a pain problem

00:51:43.137 --> 00:51:45.853
and hypnosis is very helpful for pain.

00:51:47.210 --> 00:51:50.360
And so, what I'm doing is,

00:51:50.360 --> 00:51:52.270
identifying how hypnotizable they are.

00:51:52.270 --> 00:51:53.950
I give them a standard brief test

00:51:53.950 --> 00:51:56.190
of their ability to experience hypnosis,

00:51:56.190 --> 00:51:59.480
and then going through a
self-hypnosis exercise with them

00:51:59.480 --> 00:52:02.260
to deal with the problem,
seeing how they respond to it,

00:52:02.260 --> 00:52:04.980
and then teaching them how
to do it for themselves.

00:52:04.980 --> 00:52:06.030
And in the old days,

00:52:06.030 --> 00:52:09.010
I used to have them use
their iPhone and record

00:52:10.910 --> 00:52:12.090
that part of the session.

00:52:12.090 --> 00:52:15.050
So they could play back
the hypnosis experience.

00:52:15.050 --> 00:52:18.480
Now we've developed an app, "Reveri,"

00:52:18.480 --> 00:52:22.780
that can teach people
and step them through,

00:52:22.780 --> 00:52:24.760
dealing with pain, stress,

00:52:24.760 --> 00:52:29.330
focus, insomnia and help people eat better

00:52:29.330 --> 00:52:30.823
and stop smoking.

00:52:32.040 --> 00:52:35.280
But we have elements that
take about 15 minutes

00:52:35.280 --> 00:52:37.170
and elements that just
take one or two minutes

00:52:37.170 --> 00:52:39.353
that people can refresh and reinforce.

00:52:39.353 --> 00:52:40.409
- Two minute hypnosis,

00:52:40.409 --> 00:52:41.360
[indistinct] one minute.
- Yes.

00:52:41.360 --> 00:52:42.960
- Yeah.
- And it's one to two,

00:52:42.960 --> 00:52:44.670
we're one to two minutes now,

00:52:44.670 --> 00:52:46.663
and we're finding that two
thirds of the people find

00:52:46.663 --> 00:52:49.710
that even just the one minute refresher,

00:52:49.710 --> 00:52:50.870
helps them feel better.

00:52:50.870 --> 00:52:52.120
They're reporting, they feel better.

00:52:52.120 --> 00:52:53.940
So, the nice thing is,
you know right away,

00:52:53.940 --> 00:52:56.780
whether it's likely to help you or not.

00:52:56.780 --> 00:52:59.170
And we've found, we've done studies,

00:52:59.170 --> 00:53:01.420
looking at hypnosis for pain relief

00:53:01.420 --> 00:53:03.350
in acute medical procedures.

00:53:03.350 --> 00:53:06.580
We did a randomized trial that
we published in The Lancet,

00:53:06.580 --> 00:53:09.100
three conditions, people
getting arterial cutdowns

00:53:10.094 --> 00:53:12.410
to chemoembolized tumors in the liver

00:53:12.410 --> 00:53:14.740
or visualize renal artery stenosis.

00:53:14.740 --> 00:53:16.330
You don't use general anesthesia for this.

00:53:16.330 --> 00:53:18.410
It's very uncomfortable
and people are anxious

00:53:18.410 --> 00:53:19.690
and we had three conditions.

00:53:19.690 --> 00:53:20.830
One was standard care,

00:53:20.830 --> 00:53:23.763
they could push a button
and get opioids, IV.

00:53:24.933 --> 00:53:26.760
- This is during the surgery?
- During the surgery.

00:53:26.760 --> 00:53:28.240
The second is, they could do that,

00:53:28.240 --> 00:53:30.680
plus they had a friendly
nurse, comforting them.

00:53:30.680 --> 00:53:33.560
So, we controlled for pleasant
attention and support.

00:53:33.560 --> 00:53:35.760
And the third was, we
taught them self-hypnosis

00:53:35.760 --> 00:53:36.593
for pain control.

00:53:36.593 --> 00:53:39.500
So, you're feeling, you
can change the temperature,

00:53:39.500 --> 00:53:41.640
you're your body is
cool, tingling, and numb,

00:53:41.640 --> 00:53:44.573
you're floating in ice water
and feeling comfortable.

00:53:45.890 --> 00:53:47.060
Or go somewhere else,

00:53:47.060 --> 00:53:49.750
leave your body here and
go to a desert island

00:53:49.750 --> 00:53:51.780
and enjoy yourself.

00:53:51.780 --> 00:53:53.490
And we found that,

00:53:53.490 --> 00:53:55.910
it's about two and a half hour procedure,

00:53:55.910 --> 00:53:58.420
that by an hour and a half,

00:53:58.420 --> 00:54:02.950
the hypnosis group had
reduced their pain by 80%,

00:54:02.950 --> 00:54:04.010
compared to the standard,
- Wow.

00:54:04.010 --> 00:54:07.150
care group, using half
the amount of opioids,

00:54:07.150 --> 00:54:09.080
they had fewer complications

00:54:09.080 --> 00:54:11.700
and the procedure took
17 minutes less time

00:54:11.700 --> 00:54:12.790
on average to get done.

00:54:12.790 --> 00:54:14.650
Because not only was the
patient more relaxed.

00:54:14.650 --> 00:54:16.310
So was the treatment staff.

00:54:16.310 --> 00:54:17.580
They weren't dealing with someone

00:54:17.580 --> 00:54:19.530
who's struggling and uncomfortable.

00:54:19.530 --> 00:54:22.050
We measured their anxiety and same thing.

00:54:22.050 --> 00:54:23.920
They hypnosis group, I was
worried they were all dead.

00:54:23.920 --> 00:54:25.990
They had no anxiety
after an hour and a half,

00:54:25.990 --> 00:54:27.660
they were saying, I'm fine, you know,

00:54:27.660 --> 00:54:28.970
and they were fine.

00:54:28.970 --> 00:54:30.900
And the standard care group,

00:54:30.900 --> 00:54:33.860
had 5 out of 10 anxiety
scores at that point.

00:54:33.860 --> 00:54:36.280
So, we published that in The Lancet,

00:54:36.280 --> 00:54:38.480
big randomized trial.

00:54:38.480 --> 00:54:40.870
If we had a drug that did that,

00:54:40.870 --> 00:54:44.310
every hospital in the country
would be using it now,

00:54:44.310 --> 00:54:46.290
but there's no industry to push it.

00:54:46.290 --> 00:54:48.870
So, that's part of what helped us decide

00:54:48.870 --> 00:54:51.170
that we needed to help people,

00:54:51.170 --> 00:54:52.720
do this with Reveri

00:54:52.720 --> 00:54:54.220
and teach them how to do it

00:54:54.220 --> 00:54:57.840
and provide interactive
support for them to do it.

00:54:57.840 --> 00:55:00.720
And does it, the question, although is,

00:55:00.720 --> 00:55:02.000
does it work long term?

00:55:02.000 --> 00:55:05.080
'Cause what we can do acutely
doesn't necessarily carry on.

00:55:05.080 --> 00:55:07.690
So, we did around
randomized trial of women

00:55:07.690 --> 00:55:09.010
with metastatic breast cancer.

00:55:09.010 --> 00:55:10.940
They had advancing disease.

00:55:10.940 --> 00:55:12.990
We met with them in a
support group once a week

00:55:12.990 --> 00:55:15.020
and taught them self-hypnosis,

00:55:15.020 --> 00:55:18.150
for stress and anxiety and
pain control at the end.

00:55:18.150 --> 00:55:20.660
And by the end of a year,

00:55:20.660 --> 00:55:23.110
the treatment group had half
the pain the control group did,

00:55:23.110 --> 00:55:25.580
on the same, in very low
amounts of medication.

00:55:25.580 --> 00:55:26.480
So, it lasts.

00:55:26.480 --> 00:55:27.750
And they would say,

00:55:27.750 --> 00:55:29.820
when I felt that pain in my chest

00:55:29.820 --> 00:55:33.000
and thought it was a metastasis,
I just did the exercise.

00:55:33.000 --> 00:55:35.640
I got myself in a warm
bath and I felt fine.

00:55:35.640 --> 00:55:40.640
So, it works because it becomes
a skill that people acquire,

00:55:40.660 --> 00:55:42.040
but they can tell right away,

00:55:42.040 --> 00:55:43.300
whether it's likely to help them,

00:55:43.300 --> 00:55:46.100
working with a clinician
or now using the app

00:55:46.100 --> 00:55:50.100
or other ways of helping them
learn to use it as a skill.

00:55:50.100 --> 00:55:52.960
So, the nice thing is you
will know very quickly,

00:55:52.960 --> 00:55:54.420
whether it's likely to help you or not.

00:55:54.420 --> 00:55:57.110
And if it is, you can learn
to do it for yourself.

00:55:57.110 --> 00:55:59.220
- That's great and we will,

00:55:59.220 --> 00:56:01.750
again, there'll be a link
to Reveri in the caption,

00:56:01.750 --> 00:56:04.060
it's available for Apple and Android.

00:56:04.060 --> 00:56:07.410
And I think even though
there's a nominal cost there,

00:56:07.410 --> 00:56:11.370
I think that, as you mentioned,

00:56:11.370 --> 00:56:14.190
medications and other
approaches to dealing

00:56:14.190 --> 00:56:16.860
with these problems are quite expensive,

00:56:16.860 --> 00:56:20.220
and have all the potential
for side effects and things.

00:56:20.220 --> 00:56:22.140
Not that some of those aren't also useful.

00:56:22.140 --> 00:56:23.371
- Could I, before you get to that,

00:56:23.371 --> 00:56:24.800
- Please.
- just one thing.

00:56:24.800 --> 00:56:27.070
We've worked very hard on the app.

00:56:27.070 --> 00:56:29.640
We have an iOS app for Apple.

00:56:29.640 --> 00:56:33.500
We decided to table for a
moment, redoing the Android app.

00:56:33.500 --> 00:56:37.010
So, it was available,

00:56:37.010 --> 00:56:39.710
when we were working
through the Alexa platform.

00:56:39.710 --> 00:56:41.840
It's not at the moment
but it will be soon.

00:56:41.840 --> 00:56:44.060
So, I just don't want
people to be disappointed

00:56:44.060 --> 00:56:45.990
if they're looking for it for Android,

00:56:45.990 --> 00:56:48.710
it's on our agenda but we
don't have it at the moment.

00:56:48.710 --> 00:56:50.340
- Great, thanks for that clarification.

00:56:50.340 --> 00:56:52.483
So hopefully, in time for both,

00:56:53.340 --> 00:56:56.730
I get asked a lot about obsessive thoughts

00:56:56.730 --> 00:56:58.760
or intrusive thoughts.

00:56:58.760 --> 00:57:01.330
I also get asked a lot about OCD.

00:57:01.330 --> 00:57:03.450
Is there any evidence that hypnosis

00:57:03.450 --> 00:57:05.410
or self-hypnosis can be used for,

00:57:05.410 --> 00:57:07.113
dealing with obsessive thoughts?

00:57:09.670 --> 00:57:12.530
- Sometimes, there are some
very obsessional people,

00:57:12.530 --> 00:57:15.060
who just turn out not to
be that hypnotizable for,

00:57:15.060 --> 00:57:17.192
yeah, and it's not random.

00:57:17.192 --> 00:57:19.530
They tend to be so over
controlling of thought,

00:57:19.530 --> 00:57:23.220
they're all busy evaluating
rather than experiencing, so-

00:57:23.220 --> 00:57:24.367
- I know a few people like that.

00:57:24.367 --> 00:57:26.823
[David laughing]

00:57:26.823 --> 00:57:30.430
It sounds like an adaptive
mindset for a lot of professions.

00:57:30.430 --> 00:57:31.263
- That's right.

00:57:31.263 --> 00:57:33.230
- And then we get trained up in that,

00:57:33.230 --> 00:57:34.920
- Yeah.
- during school,

00:57:34.920 --> 00:57:36.630
how to obsess over the exam,

00:57:36.630 --> 00:57:38.340
obsess over the,
- Yeah.

00:57:38.340 --> 00:57:40.290
- our social interactions.

00:57:40.290 --> 00:57:42.570
I mean, it's part of becoming
a functional human being.

00:57:42.570 --> 00:57:45.210
And yet, it can take us down a different-

00:57:45.210 --> 00:57:46.410
- We sometimes overdo it.

00:57:46.410 --> 00:57:49.423
I mean, I'll tell you one
example from extreme situations,

00:57:50.560 --> 00:57:52.810
that, you know, you're
judging, evaluating,

00:57:52.810 --> 00:57:54.250
you're not letting yourself experience,

00:57:54.250 --> 00:57:55.710
including emotionally.

00:57:55.710 --> 00:57:57.450
I know somebody,

00:57:57.450 --> 00:58:00.760
who listens to the tapes
from airplanes that go down.

00:58:00.760 --> 00:58:03.350
So, they get the black
box and they listen to it.

00:58:03.350 --> 00:58:04.830
And he said to me, you know-

00:58:04.830 --> 00:58:06.357
- That's his profession or
he does this recreational-

00:58:06.357 --> 00:58:07.407
- No, it's his profession.
- Okay.

00:58:07.407 --> 00:58:09.430
- That's what he did.

00:58:09.430 --> 00:58:11.210
'Cause they're trying to
do accident prevention

00:58:11.210 --> 00:58:12.400
and how to handle things.

00:58:12.400 --> 00:58:17.400
And he said that you worry
about people panicking, right?

00:58:17.410 --> 00:58:20.760
And here these guys know
that they've got 30 seconds

00:58:20.760 --> 00:58:22.460
or some 45 seconds

00:58:22.460 --> 00:58:24.490
and they're just going
through their checklist.

00:58:24.490 --> 00:58:27.000
He said, they don't panic enough.

00:58:27.000 --> 00:58:28.747
They're taught that this is what you do.

00:58:28.747 --> 00:58:31.350
And there is reason,
there's good reason for it.

00:58:31.350 --> 00:58:33.560
But sometimes they overdo it.

00:58:33.560 --> 00:58:36.340
And it's painful to listen to this

00:58:36.340 --> 00:58:37.490
'cause you know what's going to happen.

00:58:37.490 --> 00:58:40.260
So, it's kind of a balance we have to hit.

00:58:40.260 --> 00:58:43.560
And sometimes we get too
emotional and too absorbed

00:58:43.560 --> 00:58:46.150
and you're not with that enough

00:58:46.150 --> 00:58:48.260
to sort of see other possibilities.

00:58:48.260 --> 00:58:49.100
That can be a problem.

00:58:49.100 --> 00:58:49.933
But on the other hand,

00:58:49.933 --> 00:58:52.040
sometimes you're too rigid and controlled

00:58:52.040 --> 00:58:54.460
and you don't let your emotions guide you

00:58:54.460 --> 00:58:56.820
to what you need to do to protect yourself

00:58:56.820 --> 00:58:57.670
or protect others.

00:58:57.670 --> 00:59:02.373
So, I would say in general
that people with OCD are,

00:59:03.483 --> 00:59:05.840
on the less hypnotizable
side of the spectrum,

00:59:05.840 --> 00:59:08.460
they're less likely to allow
themselves to engage in any,

00:59:08.460 --> 00:59:10.590
and the typical example is the checking

00:59:10.590 --> 00:59:12.180
with OCD for example,

00:59:12.180 --> 00:59:13.743
they don't remember,

00:59:14.850 --> 00:59:17.030
whether they locked the door

00:59:17.030 --> 00:59:19.380
or turned off the gas in the
oven and they keep going back

00:59:19.380 --> 00:59:20.213
and they keep checking.

00:59:20.213 --> 00:59:23.560
So, there the evaluative
component of the brain,

00:59:23.560 --> 00:59:26.160
kind of overrides the experiential one

00:59:26.160 --> 00:59:29.350
and sometimes people can get some benefit,

00:59:29.350 --> 00:59:32.430
but they're not a group
that I would select

00:59:32.430 --> 00:59:35.100
for being the most likely to respond

00:59:35.100 --> 00:59:37.140
to self hypnotic approaches.

00:59:37.140 --> 00:59:39.083
- Are superstitions similar?

00:59:40.520 --> 00:59:44.250
- Superstitions, I think that's more,

00:59:44.250 --> 00:59:47.780
there are people who
are very hypnotizable,

00:59:47.780 --> 00:59:51.050
who keep getting caught up
in things like superstitions

00:59:51.050 --> 00:59:55.230
and there, the imagination
supplants the reality.

00:59:55.230 --> 00:59:58.120
And we've seen a lot of
that happening recently.

00:59:58.120 --> 01:00:01.270
And so I think there,

01:00:01.270 --> 01:00:03.810
it's by possible that they
could be helped by learning

01:00:03.810 --> 01:00:07.110
to sort of see it but put it in context,

01:00:07.110 --> 01:00:09.020
see it from a different point of view.

01:00:09.020 --> 01:00:11.090
- I developed a pretty
vicious superstition,

01:00:11.090 --> 01:00:13.940
when I was in college and it
was hard to break, actually.

01:00:14.820 --> 01:00:16.930
I always feel, when I talk to clinicians,

01:00:16.930 --> 01:00:18.210
I have to reveal certain things

01:00:18.210 --> 01:00:19.223
about my own pathology.
- Please do.

01:00:19.223 --> 01:00:20.870
- And so-
- You'll get my bill later.

01:00:20.870 --> 01:00:22.222
- Thank you, yes,

01:00:22.222 --> 01:00:23.078
it's part of the reason I arranged this.

01:00:23.078 --> 01:00:24.440
[David laughing]
No, I'm just kidding.

01:00:24.440 --> 01:00:25.273
But yeah, I did.

01:00:25.273 --> 01:00:27.643
I had a habit of knocking
on wood for things

01:00:27.643 --> 01:00:29.733
and I noticed it started to,

01:00:32.890 --> 01:00:35.510
I would sneak knocking on
wood every once in a while

01:00:35.510 --> 01:00:37.470
'cause I didn't want people to
think I was doing too often.

01:00:37.470 --> 01:00:38.650
And then I started to realize that,

01:00:38.650 --> 01:00:41.410
it was becoming a little bit of a reflex.

01:00:41.410 --> 01:00:43.790
And then I saw this incredible video

01:00:43.790 --> 01:00:45.850
from Bence Olveczky's lab at Harvard.

01:00:45.850 --> 01:00:47.480
He studies motor patterns.

01:00:47.480 --> 01:00:50.220
And he has these rats that
press different sequences

01:00:50.220 --> 01:00:54.410
of levers and turn dials in
order to get a pellet of food.

01:00:54.410 --> 01:00:55.440
But as they do that,

01:00:55.440 --> 01:00:56.980
they'll start to introduce these behaviors

01:00:56.980 --> 01:00:59.720
that have nothing to do with
the actual lever pressing,

01:00:59.720 --> 01:01:02.180
like they'll start scratching
their hind quarters

01:01:02.180 --> 01:01:03.013
and things like that.

01:01:03.013 --> 01:01:04.970
And their hat, their heads, excuse me,

01:01:04.970 --> 01:01:06.710
they don't wear hats
and flipping their ears.

01:01:06.710 --> 01:01:11.330
And this is just like a pitcher
before throwing a baseball.

01:01:11.330 --> 01:01:12.800
That we do this,
- Oh, yeah.

01:01:12.800 --> 01:01:15.170
- we start to incorporate motor behaviors

01:01:15.170 --> 01:01:17.190
that are unrelated to the outcome,

01:01:17.190 --> 01:01:19.710
but our mind somehow starts to think

01:01:19.710 --> 01:01:21.620
that they're necessary for the outcome.

01:01:21.620 --> 01:01:22.690
And so then you incorporate it.

01:01:22.690 --> 01:01:25.040
So, I decided to break it
by simply forcing myself

01:01:25.040 --> 01:01:26.340
to not do it for about a week.
- Mm-hm.

01:01:26.340 --> 01:01:28.530
And then it just seemed like a ridiculous,

01:01:28.530 --> 01:01:29.363
- [Both] thing to do.

01:01:29.363 --> 01:01:30.870
Yeah, well-
- Knock on wood.

01:01:30.870 --> 01:01:34.360
- We call that response
prevention and it works.

01:01:34.360 --> 01:01:35.510
What you do is,

01:01:35.510 --> 01:01:37.800
you set up a new context in your brain,

01:01:37.800 --> 01:01:39.610
where you get the outcome you want,

01:01:39.610 --> 01:01:42.710
devoid of the extraneous behavior.

01:01:42.710 --> 01:01:44.830
- Yeah, and I knew it was nuts, right.

01:01:44.830 --> 01:01:46.320
I knew it was illogical.
- Right.

01:01:46.320 --> 01:01:48.620
- But somehow these
things take on meaning.

01:01:48.620 --> 01:01:49.730
- [David] Right.

01:01:49.730 --> 01:01:51.450
- So, we talked about,

01:01:51.450 --> 01:01:55.210
the utility of hypnosis
for stress reduction,

01:01:55.210 --> 01:01:57.540
phobias, pain,

01:01:57.540 --> 01:01:59.800
possibly, we don't know but for,

01:01:59.800 --> 01:02:01.600
things like ADHD and OCD,

01:02:01.600 --> 01:02:04.051
it just will depend on hypnotizability.

01:02:04.051 --> 01:02:05.000
- Right.

01:02:05.000 --> 01:02:07.350
- You talked about this
beautiful study on the,

01:02:08.220 --> 01:02:13.063
metastatic breast cancer
outcome or patients.

01:02:13.063 --> 01:02:15.800
Hypnotizability is clearly a key variable.

01:02:15.800 --> 01:02:18.890
- Yes.
- So could you please tell us,

01:02:18.890 --> 01:02:20.700
what hypnotizability is,

01:02:20.700 --> 01:02:22.370
how it's evaluated

01:02:22.370 --> 01:02:25.060
and what The Spiegel Eye-Roll Test is?

01:02:25.060 --> 01:02:25.893
- Okay, sure.

01:02:25.893 --> 01:02:30.893
So, hypnotizability is just a capacity

01:02:31.360 --> 01:02:33.240
to have hypnotic experiences.

01:02:33.240 --> 01:02:35.910
And we have a test called the
hypnotic conduction profile.

01:02:35.910 --> 01:02:39.540
Where we give a highly
structured hypnotic experience.

01:02:39.540 --> 01:02:42.890
And the old tradition in
clinical hypnosis was,

01:02:42.890 --> 01:02:45.050
that you try a bunch of different things,

01:02:45.050 --> 01:02:47.360
talking, walking upstairs and downstairs

01:02:47.360 --> 01:02:50.170
and other images and time what you say

01:02:50.170 --> 01:02:52.210
to the breathing of the
subject and all that.

01:02:52.210 --> 01:02:55.900
And the more you change
what you do as a clinician,

01:02:55.900 --> 01:02:59.050
the less you can make of
variation and outcome.

01:02:59.050 --> 01:03:00.870
And it could take a long time, you know,

01:03:00.870 --> 01:03:02.840
20 minutes, 30 minutes.

01:03:02.840 --> 01:03:05.930
And I just view that as a kind of complex,

01:03:05.930 --> 01:03:07.020
not very effective way

01:03:07.020 --> 01:03:09.740
of assessing the person's
hypnotic capacity.

01:03:09.740 --> 01:03:11.950
We know that the peak period

01:03:11.950 --> 01:03:14.260
of hypnotizability in the human life,

01:03:14.260 --> 01:03:16.210
is the latency years in childhood.

01:03:16.210 --> 01:03:19.300
So, every eight year old is
in a trance all the time.

01:03:19.300 --> 01:03:20.910
You call all 'em in for
dinner, they don't hear ya,

01:03:20.910 --> 01:03:21.940
they're doing their thing.

01:03:21.940 --> 01:03:24.540
And that's why childhood is
such a wonderful experience.

01:03:24.540 --> 01:03:26.850
Work and play are all the same thing.

01:03:26.850 --> 01:03:29.080
And we try to make them
into little adults,

01:03:29.080 --> 01:03:31.320
which I think is a terrible mistake.

01:03:31.320 --> 01:03:32.560
Everything is fun for them.

01:03:32.560 --> 01:03:34.680
They enjoy learning,
they enjoy everything.

01:03:34.680 --> 01:03:36.693
- So what age are they in this-

01:03:36.693 --> 01:03:39.627
- This is like 6 to 10, 6 to 11.

01:03:39.627 --> 01:03:42.430
And they're playful,
they enjoy everything.

01:03:42.430 --> 01:03:44.640
Everything is sort of a game and fun

01:03:44.640 --> 01:03:46.450
and we try to make it miserable for them,

01:03:46.450 --> 01:03:47.970
but they've got it.

01:03:47.970 --> 01:03:50.423
And then when, what Piaget called,

01:03:51.662 --> 01:03:53.670
a more adult cognitive framework,

01:03:53.670 --> 01:03:56.320
where we learn abstract concepts,

01:03:56.320 --> 01:03:58.280
we learn that even if
one bottle looks bigger

01:03:58.280 --> 01:04:00.050
than the other, they
can have equal volume.

01:04:00.050 --> 01:04:02.070
And so, we at imposing logic,

01:04:02.070 --> 01:04:04.790
we're growing our DLPFC at that point

01:04:04.790 --> 01:04:07.963
and imposing cognitive
structure on experience.

01:04:08.920 --> 01:04:12.430
Some people start to lose
that hypnotic ability.

01:04:12.430 --> 01:04:14.803
By the time you're in your early 20s,

01:04:16.490 --> 01:04:19.940
your hypnotizability
becomes extremely fixed.

01:04:19.940 --> 01:04:22.600
And there was a study done at Stanford,

01:04:22.600 --> 01:04:25.853
Ernest Hillard, Phil Zimbardo
did this, looking at,

01:04:27.620 --> 01:04:31.080
they've tracked down students
who were in psych one,

01:04:31.080 --> 01:04:32.920
had their hypnotizability measured

01:04:32.920 --> 01:04:35.830
and retested them blindly 25 years later.

01:04:35.830 --> 01:04:38.200
And the test, retest correlation was,

01:04:38.200 --> 01:04:40.240
you want to guess what it was?

01:04:40.240 --> 01:04:44.860
- I'm guessing it's, I
don't know, 0.6 something.

01:04:44.860 --> 01:04:46.199
- Yeah, very close.

01:04:46.199 --> 01:04:49.110
It was 0.7, IQ would be 0.6,

01:04:49.110 --> 01:04:50.240
on the 25 year interval.
- Wow.

01:04:50.240 --> 01:04:53.150
- So, it's more stable than
IQ over a 25 year interval.

01:04:53.150 --> 01:04:57.070
So, once you're at that
point, that's where you are.

01:04:57.070 --> 01:04:59.560
What are the factors that lead to that?

01:04:59.560 --> 01:05:01.080
Well, and so what it means is that,

01:05:01.080 --> 01:05:04.280
about a third of that adults
are just not hypnotizable.

01:05:04.280 --> 01:05:05.380
Two thirds are,

01:05:05.380 --> 01:05:07.770
about 15% are extremely hypnotizable

01:05:07.770 --> 01:05:09.350
and we can measure that
and give it a number

01:05:09.350 --> 01:05:11.040
from 0 to 10.

01:05:11.040 --> 01:05:12.370
And that's very useful.

01:05:12.370 --> 01:05:14.350
For some of my patients when I do it,

01:05:14.350 --> 01:05:16.070
I say, look, I'm sorry,
you're not hypnotizable

01:05:16.070 --> 01:05:17.810
but we're going to do something else.

01:05:17.810 --> 01:05:20.560
Medication, systematic desensitization,

01:05:20.560 --> 01:05:21.880
mindfulness, other things

01:05:22.750 --> 01:05:25.940
or if they're very
hypnotizable, I just go for it.

01:05:25.940 --> 01:05:27.560
I don't do a lot of explaining.

01:05:27.560 --> 01:05:30.130
People who are low to
moderate hypnotizable,

01:05:30.130 --> 01:05:31.960
like explanations about what you're doing,

01:05:31.960 --> 01:05:34.170
but then they can still get the benefits.

