can suppress amygdala activity

01:16:21.860 --> 01:16:25.610
in some animals and animal
models and in humans as well.

01:16:25.610 --> 01:16:27.950
But it really hasn't been
explored much, neurally.

01:16:27.950 --> 01:16:28.783
I've heard things like,

01:16:28.783 --> 01:16:30.540
it coordinates the two sides of the brain,

01:16:30.540 --> 01:16:33.330
which it, to me is just a throwaway.

01:16:33.330 --> 01:16:34.950
I don't think there's any evidence that,

01:16:34.950 --> 01:16:36.530
coordinating the two sides of the brain

01:16:36.530 --> 01:16:38.350
is better than not coordinating.

01:16:38.350 --> 01:16:39.440
I wouldn't be speaking right now

01:16:39.440 --> 01:16:40.840
if the two sides of my brain,

01:16:40.840 --> 01:16:42.320
were well correlated.
- Yeah.

01:16:42.320 --> 01:16:44.280
- Because language is lateralized so,

01:16:44.280 --> 01:16:45.850
- Right, exactly.
- I heard that it mimics,

01:16:45.850 --> 01:16:48.560
rapid eye movements during
sleep, but actually it doesn't.

01:16:48.560 --> 01:16:50.120
So,
- Right.

01:16:50.120 --> 01:16:52.380
- But I have heard people talk

01:16:52.380 --> 01:16:55.280
about their positive
experiences with EMDR.

01:16:55.280 --> 01:16:56.770
What are your thoughts about EMDR?

01:16:56.770 --> 01:16:58.027
- Yeah, you had a good comment on that

01:16:58.027 --> 01:16:59.830
in one of your recent podcasts.

01:16:59.830 --> 01:17:02.610
And I'll tell you,

01:17:02.610 --> 01:17:04.900
one way I sort of think about it

01:17:04.900 --> 01:17:07.030
from a bemused point of view,

01:17:07.030 --> 01:17:08.880
is the old, you mentioned it earlier,

01:17:08.880 --> 01:17:12.130
the oldest sort of idea
of a hypnotic induction,

01:17:12.130 --> 01:17:14.450
was a dangling watch, right?

01:17:14.450 --> 01:17:16.330
And [indistinct] watch.

01:17:16.330 --> 01:17:19.640
And, in fact, there was
enough concern about it

01:17:19.640 --> 01:17:21.690
that when automobiles were invented,

01:17:21.690 --> 01:17:25.970
there was a movement to prevent
installing windshield wipers

01:17:25.970 --> 01:17:29.620
because people were afraid
that they would be hypnotized,

01:17:29.620 --> 01:17:32.440
if they watched the windshield
wipers go back and forth

01:17:32.440 --> 01:17:33.610
on a car.

01:17:33.610 --> 01:17:35.670
Now, it turns out fortunately that,

01:17:35.670 --> 01:17:37.530
you tend not to look at
the windshield wipers.

01:17:37.530 --> 01:17:39.940
You keep looking through the windshield.

01:17:39.940 --> 01:17:42.770
And so, we have windshield wipers today.

01:17:42.770 --> 01:17:44.550
But that movement is,

01:17:44.550 --> 01:17:46.790
what exactly used to be
a hypnotic induction.

01:17:46.790 --> 01:17:50.330
I think there is a lot
of hypnosis in EMDR.

01:17:50.330 --> 01:17:51.163
- Ah.
- And I think,

01:17:51.163 --> 01:17:55.780
it's a combination of that
with exposure based treatments,

01:17:55.780 --> 01:17:57.810
where you use EMDR to think about it.

01:17:57.810 --> 01:18:01.020
You tend not to process
the experience as much

01:18:01.020 --> 01:18:03.130
and just do the physical part of it,

01:18:03.130 --> 01:18:05.370
which I personally think is a drawback.

01:18:05.370 --> 01:18:09.360
And, every study I've seen
that was a dismantling study.

01:18:09.360 --> 01:18:11.780
There's no question that
people who go through EMDR,

01:18:11.780 --> 01:18:14.447
many of them get better
with trauma related problems

01:18:14.447 --> 01:18:17.490
and the VA has a big
program using it and so on.

01:18:17.490 --> 01:18:20.620
But every program that has dismantled,

01:18:20.620 --> 01:18:21.840
going through the treatment

01:18:21.840 --> 01:18:24.680
with having the lateral eye movement,

01:18:24.680 --> 01:18:26.400
has shown that the lateral eye movement,

01:18:26.400 --> 01:18:27.980
doesn't add anything to it.

01:18:27.980 --> 01:18:29.890
And toward the end of her career,

01:18:29.890 --> 01:18:33.940
Francine was doing [indistinct]
contralateral touching

01:18:33.940 --> 01:18:35.910
or something, it wasn't
eye movements anymore.

01:18:35.910 --> 01:18:36.743
It was other things.

01:18:36.743 --> 01:18:40.430
So, I tend to think that,

01:18:40.430 --> 01:18:45.430
EMDR is another form of exposure
based therapy for trauma,

01:18:45.700 --> 01:18:47.980
but as you've implied

01:18:47.980 --> 01:18:50.500
with the exception of
this possible new data,

01:18:50.500 --> 01:18:52.510
it certainly doesn't have to do with,

01:18:52.510 --> 01:18:53.810
rapid eye movement sleep.

01:18:53.810 --> 01:18:56.450
And I don't think moving
the eyes is the issue.

01:18:56.450 --> 01:18:59.560
I think it's a way of sitting
down and confronting trauma.

01:18:59.560 --> 01:19:00.690
And I would rather,

01:19:00.690 --> 01:19:02.840
that the trauma itself
be processed a bit more

01:19:02.840 --> 01:19:05.150
than often happens in EMDR.

01:19:05.150 --> 01:19:07.240
So, a lot of people have gotten therapy.

01:19:07.240 --> 01:19:08.530
Some of them have been helped.

01:19:08.530 --> 01:19:10.520
Francine used to originally claim

01:19:10.520 --> 01:19:13.520
that just one session would
desensitize people and do it.

01:19:13.520 --> 01:19:14.980
And that's clearly not true.

01:19:14.980 --> 01:19:16.530
I see a lot of people who said,

01:19:16.530 --> 01:19:18.830
yeah, it helped for a
while but I need more.

01:19:18.830 --> 01:19:23.313
So, I think it became a kind of a,

01:19:24.460 --> 01:19:28.170
overly simplistic approach to
understanding brain physiology

01:19:28.170 --> 01:19:30.410
and that part is wrong.

01:19:30.410 --> 01:19:32.970
And the interesting thing,

01:19:32.970 --> 01:19:35.700
you mentioned suppressing
amygdala activity.

01:19:35.700 --> 01:19:39.470
It's very interesting that,
my late friend Allen Hobson,

01:19:39.470 --> 01:19:40.770
who was a brilliant sleep researcher,

01:19:40.770 --> 01:19:42.150
you know Alan-
- Sleep researcher.

01:19:42.150 --> 01:19:42.983
Well, I don't know him,

01:19:42.983 --> 01:19:45.040
but I read his book when I was in college

01:19:45.040 --> 01:19:46.860
about the chemistry of sleep.
- Right.

01:19:46.860 --> 01:19:50.470
And the similarities between dream states

01:19:50.470 --> 01:19:52.207
and hallucinations.
- Yes.

01:19:52.207 --> 01:19:54.530
- And it's one of the reasons
I got into this business.

01:19:54.530 --> 01:19:55.920
- Yes, well, I worked with him

01:19:55.920 --> 01:19:57.483
in a MacArthur mind-body
network for many years.

01:19:57.483 --> 01:19:59.630
- Wow.
- He was a brilliant guy,

01:19:59.630 --> 01:20:02.390
points out that we need to get into,

01:20:02.390 --> 01:20:05.440
primarily a parasympathetic
state to go to sleep,

01:20:05.440 --> 01:20:09.700
that we have to shut off the
sympathetic nervous system.

01:20:09.700 --> 01:20:11.580
And that's why a loud noise wakes you up,

01:20:11.580 --> 01:20:13.510
when your heart rate goes up and all this.

01:20:13.510 --> 01:20:16.500
So, he was brilliant at documenting

01:20:16.500 --> 01:20:17.700
what happens in the brain at sleep.

01:20:17.700 --> 01:20:20.830
He pointed out something also
very interesting about dreams,

01:20:20.830 --> 01:20:23.750
which is that the stories in dreams,

01:20:23.750 --> 01:20:26.190
and even the images in dreams
can change all over the place

01:20:26.190 --> 01:20:27.450
in crazy ways.

01:20:27.450 --> 01:20:30.515
But usually the affect is constant,

01:20:30.515 --> 01:20:32.690
he said, usually if it's
a frustration dream,

01:20:32.690 --> 01:20:34.600
whatever happens, you end up frustrated.

01:20:34.600 --> 01:20:36.860
And if it's a enjoyment dream,

01:20:36.860 --> 01:20:38.670
you enjoy whatever's going on.

01:20:38.670 --> 01:20:42.220
So, there's an odd consistency
and affect in dreams

01:20:42.220 --> 01:20:44.970
that you don't have in other states.

01:20:44.970 --> 01:20:47.890
And the idea of lateral eye movement,

01:20:47.890 --> 01:20:50.350
suppressing amygdala activity
would kind of fit with that,

01:20:50.350 --> 01:20:52.610
that you don't allow intrusions

01:20:52.610 --> 01:20:55.610
of fear and anger and upset in dreams.

01:20:55.610 --> 01:20:56.980
It may be there all the time,

01:20:56.980 --> 01:20:59.650
but it may not be there
when you think it should be.

01:20:59.650 --> 01:21:02.090
So, why is it that you can
be falling off a building

01:21:02.090 --> 01:21:03.790
and somehow not that scared,

01:21:03.790 --> 01:21:06.840
you're just having this
experience of flying in a dream.

01:21:06.840 --> 01:21:10.980
So, I think there may
be something going on

01:21:10.980 --> 01:21:12.910
about regulating affect, but there are,

01:21:12.910 --> 01:21:16.700
we have elaborated better
ways to regulate affect.

01:21:16.700 --> 01:21:18.670
- Great, so EMDR,

01:21:18.670 --> 01:21:21.200
might incorporate some
elements of hypnosis,

01:21:21.200 --> 01:21:22.920
so the lateralized eye movements,

01:21:22.920 --> 01:21:24.810
perhaps by way of
suppressing the amygdala,

01:21:24.810 --> 01:21:26.030
this fear associated center,

01:21:26.030 --> 01:21:29.240
might bring people into a more
parasympathetic calm state.

01:21:29.240 --> 01:21:30.990
So, it might be pseudo hypnosis,

01:21:30.990 --> 01:21:32.150
and then an exposure therapy

01:21:32.150 --> 01:21:34.320
through the discussion about the issue.

01:21:34.320 --> 01:21:35.710
- Right.
- Okay.

01:21:35.710 --> 01:21:38.410
More research needed on EMDR out there.

01:21:38.410 --> 01:21:40.453
And obviously,

01:21:41.550 --> 01:21:43.540
something that's come up
a lot in this discussion

01:21:43.540 --> 01:21:46.470
and in our discussions that,

01:21:46.470 --> 01:21:49.640
have the great fortune of
talking to you every week is,

01:21:49.640 --> 01:21:51.660
and working together is,

01:21:51.660 --> 01:21:56.380
this idea of getting close to the phobia,

01:21:56.380 --> 01:21:57.740
getting close to the trauma,

01:21:57.740 --> 01:22:00.080
re-experiencing it as a portal

01:22:00.080 --> 01:22:03.920
to then adjusting the response
to it and rewiring something.

01:22:03.920 --> 01:22:05.720
So, the troubling thing

01:22:05.720 --> 01:22:09.740
or the horrible thing is no
longer as horrible to us.

01:22:09.740 --> 01:22:14.620
but the repeating theme is we can't expect

01:22:14.620 --> 01:22:17.160
to get over something without
getting really close to it.

01:22:17.160 --> 01:22:20.423
Maybe even experiencing it somatically.

01:22:21.440 --> 01:22:23.050
Nowadays, we hear a lot about,

01:22:23.050 --> 01:22:24.760
triggers and trigger warnings.

01:22:24.760 --> 01:22:29.760
And certainly, one can
understand why those exist,

01:22:29.890 --> 01:22:32.700
but it seems like in
the general population,

01:22:32.700 --> 01:22:35.120
there's this idea that
we want to move away

01:22:35.120 --> 01:22:36.850
from anything that upsets us and yet,

01:22:36.850 --> 01:22:38.240
- Right.
- I think it's fair to say,

01:22:38.240 --> 01:22:40.410
even though I haven't
gathered the statistics that,

01:22:40.410 --> 01:22:41.660
on the whole,

01:22:41.660 --> 01:22:44.480
that human beings are
becoming more and more anxious

01:22:44.480 --> 01:22:45.830
and more and more stressed,

01:22:46.860 --> 01:22:50.580
perhaps because of but
certainly in parallel,

01:22:50.580 --> 01:22:52.870
with the fact that we're
trying to move away

01:22:52.870 --> 01:22:54.530
from troubling things.

01:22:54.530 --> 01:22:58.050
So, I've heard you say before,

01:22:58.050 --> 01:23:01.200
that in terms of therapeutic approaches,

01:23:01.200 --> 01:23:03.420
it's not just about
the state you get into,

01:23:03.420 --> 01:23:06.960
but whether or not you brought
yourself there voluntarily.

01:23:06.960 --> 01:23:07.830
- That's exactly right.

01:23:07.830 --> 01:23:11.500
- So, this element of
deliberate self exposure,

01:23:11.500 --> 01:23:14.370
deciding I'm going to confront the trauma,

01:23:14.370 --> 01:23:16.030
I'm going to confront the pain.

01:23:16.030 --> 01:23:18.080
I'm going to confront the insomnia.

01:23:18.080 --> 01:23:19.450
I'm going to confront the, you know,

01:23:19.450 --> 01:23:20.830
and fill in the blank.

01:23:20.830 --> 01:23:24.713
And then readjusting
one's emotional response,

01:23:25.760 --> 01:23:28.100
right up next to that troubling thing.

01:23:28.100 --> 01:23:31.290
That seems to be the
hallmark of this treatment.

01:23:31.290 --> 01:23:32.123
And,

01:23:33.290 --> 01:23:34.610
if I'm thinking about it correctly,

01:23:34.610 --> 01:23:37.623
of pretty much all treatments
for getting over stuff.

01:23:38.670 --> 01:23:40.100
If people don't have access

01:23:40.100 --> 01:23:42.650
to a really good clinician, like yourself,

01:23:42.650 --> 01:23:45.940
how should they carry these
thoughts and these ideas?

01:23:45.940 --> 01:23:49.260
I mean, I think almost
everybody of any reasonable age,

01:23:49.260 --> 01:23:52.300
has memories or things that upset them,

01:23:52.300 --> 01:23:53.923
but we learn to suppress them.

01:23:55.030 --> 01:23:56.840
What does one do?

01:23:56.840 --> 01:23:58.630
Obviously, the Reveri app has approaches

01:23:58.630 --> 01:24:02.510
to dealing with some of
this, inside of the app,

01:24:02.510 --> 01:24:04.320
but how does one start to think about,

01:24:04.320 --> 01:24:06.700
actually dealing with something like this

01:24:06.700 --> 01:24:08.080
and avoiding the hazards

01:24:08.080 --> 01:24:10.910
of just kind of reactivating
a lot of painful experiences?

01:24:10.910 --> 01:24:12.810
Because a lot of being
a functional human being

01:24:12.810 --> 01:24:14.630
is also going to work each day,

01:24:14.630 --> 01:24:17.270
interacting with people and
not bringing one's trauma,

01:24:17.270 --> 01:24:19.140
and dumping it out all on the table

01:24:19.140 --> 01:24:22.810
or being able to just
function is so crucial.

01:24:22.810 --> 01:24:25.490
So, how do you think
about this as a clinician?

01:24:25.490 --> 01:24:27.460
- Well, the image it comes to mind

01:24:27.460 --> 01:24:29.590
is the Greek myth of Pandora's Box.

01:24:29.590 --> 01:24:31.360
That it opened and the Furies got out

01:24:31.360 --> 01:24:33.300
and you couldn't put 'em back in.

01:24:33.300 --> 01:24:35.100
And we have this kind of fantasy

01:24:35.100 --> 01:24:37.610
that once you get into these memories,

01:24:37.610 --> 01:24:38.460
they'll take you over

01:24:38.460 --> 01:24:40.580
and you'll never get them back in the box.

01:24:40.580 --> 01:24:42.163
And I think that's wrong.

01:24:43.250 --> 01:24:45.250
People who use hypnosis say that,

01:24:45.250 --> 01:24:47.700
there are ways to present things to people

01:24:47.700 --> 01:24:49.680
that will be helpful and ways that won't.

01:24:49.680 --> 01:24:51.810
And one real mistake is to tell someone,

01:24:51.810 --> 01:24:54.330
don't think about purple elephants.

01:24:54.330 --> 01:24:55.530
What are you thinking about?
- Purple elephants.

01:24:55.530 --> 01:24:56.600
- It doesn't work.

01:24:56.600 --> 01:25:01.130
So, you want to find a way to
feel in control of the access

01:25:01.130 --> 01:25:04.460
and to define what
happened on your own terms.

01:25:04.460 --> 01:25:06.880
And so, I'm not a big
fan of trigger warnings.

01:25:06.880 --> 01:25:09.040
I think we're going crazy over,

01:25:09.040 --> 01:25:10.780
this could be upsetting,
that could be upsetting.

01:25:10.780 --> 01:25:12.950
Yeah, there are lots of
things that are upsetting.

01:25:12.950 --> 01:25:16.240
The average kid has
watched 20,000 murders,

01:25:16.240 --> 01:25:17.720
by the time he's 20 years old,

01:25:17.720 --> 01:25:20.280
watching television and movies these days.

01:25:20.280 --> 01:25:22.690
So, we see terrible things

01:25:22.690 --> 01:25:24.340
and it's not a matter of,

01:25:24.340 --> 01:25:26.860
are you exposed to
something that's upsetting,

01:25:26.860 --> 01:25:28.090
but how do you handle it?

01:25:28.090 --> 01:25:30.370
What do you make of it and
are you feeling in control?

01:25:30.370 --> 01:25:31.293
It's not like,

01:25:32.926 --> 01:25:36.700
what Putin is doing to
his rival in Russia,

01:25:36.700 --> 01:25:39.360
forcing him to watch propaganda
movies 10 hours a day,

01:25:39.360 --> 01:25:40.770
while he is in prison.

01:25:40.770 --> 01:25:45.770
It's a matter of thinking about a problem,

01:25:46.340 --> 01:25:49.170
in a way that leaves you
feeling, you understand better.

01:25:49.170 --> 01:25:52.640
You're in more control, you
can turn it off when you want,

01:25:52.640 --> 01:25:54.260
you can turn it on when you want.

01:25:54.260 --> 01:25:57.290
And so we have to, in life,
deal with stressful things,

01:25:57.290 --> 01:25:58.460
there are studies,

01:25:58.460 --> 01:26:01.980
Karen Parker at Stanford has
done some wonderful studies

01:26:01.980 --> 01:26:04.050
with primates about stress inoculation.

01:26:04.050 --> 01:26:07.990
That if you separate a
baby monkey from his mother

01:26:07.990 --> 01:26:10.620
for two hours a day and then reunite them,

01:26:10.620 --> 01:26:13.280
and then you stress
that baby monkey later,

01:26:13.280 --> 01:26:14.890
they actually handle stress better.

01:26:14.890 --> 01:26:17.610
There's less cortisol arousal
in the face of distress,

01:26:17.610 --> 01:26:19.350
stress inoculation, that's been called.

01:26:19.350 --> 01:26:23.270
So, mere exposure to trauma or stress.

01:26:23.270 --> 01:26:24.860
It's a part of living anyway.

01:26:24.860 --> 01:26:27.140
We can't avoid it, even if we'd like to,

01:26:27.140 --> 01:26:28.670
and,

01:26:28.670 --> 01:26:30.210
it's not pleasant, it's not great

01:26:30.210 --> 01:26:32.690
but it's sometimes things
you need to learn about life.

01:26:32.690 --> 01:26:36.410
And if you can find an
algorithm for facing it,

01:26:36.410 --> 01:26:38.450
putting it into perspective,
dealing with it,

01:26:38.450 --> 01:26:40.830
you become a stronger
person not a weaker person.

01:26:40.830 --> 01:26:41.890
So, this idea that,

01:26:41.890 --> 01:26:44.690
college students are such fragile flowers,

01:26:44.690 --> 01:26:47.260
that if you talk about a
sexual assault or something,

01:26:47.260 --> 01:26:49.320
you're doing something terrible to them,

01:26:49.320 --> 01:26:50.340
it's just wrong.

01:26:50.340 --> 01:26:54.460
And I think we need to build our ability

01:26:54.460 --> 01:26:56.260
to recognize and manage stress.

01:26:56.260 --> 01:26:58.210
And you can't do that without doing it.

01:26:59.194 --> 01:27:00.380
You can't ride a bicycle

01:27:00.380 --> 01:27:02.340
without taking the risk of falling off it.

01:27:02.340 --> 01:27:04.960
And so, I think that's the way,

01:27:04.960 --> 01:27:07.320
I think of dealing with stress.

01:27:07.320 --> 01:27:10.220
- Yeah, I really
appreciate you saying that.

01:27:10.220 --> 01:27:12.160
You and I were both at a gathering,

01:27:12.160 --> 01:27:14.920
let's say where this
issue was being discussed,

01:27:14.920 --> 01:27:18.720
and around an issue of a
publicized sexual trauma.

01:27:18.720 --> 01:27:20.670
And you made an excellent case

01:27:20.670 --> 01:27:24.410
for why this stuff can't
be pushed under the rug.

01:27:24.410 --> 01:27:27.530
And that actually, in my observation,

01:27:27.530 --> 01:27:29.277
led to a lot of healing
for the people that

01:27:29.277 --> 01:27:32.630
and the families of people
that suffered from this.

01:27:32.630 --> 01:27:35.280
I do think people are resilient.

01:27:35.280 --> 01:27:39.923
But we don't really teach
how to think about feelings.

01:27:41.210 --> 01:27:43.060
We're told that we need
to feel our feelings,

01:27:43.060 --> 01:27:45.160
but then again, we are also told

01:27:45.160 --> 01:27:47.080
that feelings don't hold
all the information.

01:27:47.080 --> 01:27:48.560
And so, I think that as you mentioned,

01:27:48.560 --> 01:27:51.220
there's no operating or users manual

01:27:51.220 --> 01:27:53.340
for this nervous system thing.

01:27:53.340 --> 01:27:54.550
Brings me to another issue,

01:27:54.550 --> 01:27:56.010
which is the mind-body connection.

01:27:56.010 --> 01:27:57.820
Something that we're very interested in

01:27:57.820 --> 01:27:59.863
and you've done extensive work on.

01:28:01.720 --> 01:28:02.730
We all like to think that,

01:28:02.730 --> 01:28:04.450
getting more in touch with our body,

01:28:04.450 --> 01:28:05.560
would be a great thing,

01:28:05.560 --> 01:28:06.680
learning to interocept,

01:28:06.680 --> 01:28:08.400
paying attention to
our internal landscape,

01:28:08.400 --> 01:28:09.233
would be a great thing.

01:28:09.233 --> 01:28:11.460
But as we often discuss,

01:28:11.460 --> 01:28:13.200
when we're feeling lousy,

01:28:13.200 --> 01:28:15.880
then being really in touch
with that lousy feeling,

01:28:15.880 --> 01:28:18.750
may or may not be a good thing, right?

01:28:18.750 --> 01:28:21.410
So, how should we think about mind-body?

01:28:21.410 --> 01:28:23.890
I can see examples in hypnosis,

01:28:23.890 --> 01:28:25.490
from your descriptions of hypnosis,

01:28:25.490 --> 01:28:29.920
where you want to unify
the mind-body connection.

01:28:29.920 --> 01:28:31.790
Feel what you're thinking,

01:28:31.790 --> 01:28:33.330
think what you're feeling, et cetera.

01:28:33.330 --> 01:28:36.130
But I could also point to elements

01:28:36.130 --> 01:28:37.530
within the hypnotic process

01:28:37.530 --> 01:28:40.572
in which you are actively
trying to uncouple those.

01:28:40.572 --> 01:28:41.405
- That's.

01:28:41.405 --> 01:28:43.460
- So, it sounds to me like,

01:28:43.460 --> 01:28:46.140
this whole mind-body thing
is a bit more like a car.

01:28:46.140 --> 01:28:49.790
You can't say that 40 miles
per hour is the optimal speed.

01:28:49.790 --> 01:28:51.497
It kind of depends on the road you're on

01:28:51.497 --> 01:28:53.950
and the turn you may or may not be taking.

01:28:53.950 --> 01:28:54.783
- Right.

01:28:54.783 --> 01:28:58.870
- How should we think about
mind-body in terms of,

01:28:58.870 --> 01:29:00.090
navigating daily life?

01:29:00.090 --> 01:29:02.650
What do you think is the adaptive way,

01:29:02.650 --> 01:29:05.053
to conceptualize the mind-body?

01:29:06.500 --> 01:29:08.780
It's a big question.
- It is.

01:29:08.780 --> 01:29:10.990
It's a very interesting one.

01:29:10.990 --> 01:29:15.990
I guess, I think that
it's a matter or not of,

01:29:16.260 --> 01:29:18.540
absolute control but more control,

01:29:18.540 --> 01:29:23.460
that we need to think
of our brain as a tool

01:29:23.460 --> 01:29:26.670
and our body's signals as tools as well,

01:29:26.670 --> 01:29:29.960
to help us understand what's
going on in the world,

01:29:29.960 --> 01:29:33.470
what we need, what matters,
what's important, what isn't,

01:29:33.470 --> 01:29:36.230
but also something that can be managed,

01:29:36.230 --> 01:29:39.240
not simply absorbed.

01:29:39.240 --> 01:29:43.520
And so, hypnosis I think
is a kind of limiting case,

01:29:43.520 --> 01:29:46.790
where you can push it about
as far as we can push it

01:29:46.790 --> 01:29:48.680
in terms of regulating pain.

01:29:48.680 --> 01:29:51.680
Pain is a good example of that.

01:29:51.680 --> 01:29:53.600
Obviously, you need to pay attention.

01:29:53.600 --> 01:29:54.680
If you just broke your ankle,

01:29:54.680 --> 01:29:56.800
you better pay attention
to it and get help

01:29:56.800 --> 01:29:59.360
or you're having crushing
substernal chest pain.

01:29:59.360 --> 01:30:00.860
You better do something about it.

01:30:00.860 --> 01:30:02.220
But our brain,

01:30:02.220 --> 01:30:04.530
is sort of programmed to
treat all pain signals

01:30:04.530 --> 01:30:06.260
as if they were novel pain signals.

01:30:06.260 --> 01:30:09.610
If it's a sudden new problem
that needs to be attended to.

01:30:09.610 --> 01:30:14.610
I teach people to think of
the pain and categorize it,

01:30:14.660 --> 01:30:17.870
does the pain mean that
if you put weight on this,

01:30:17.870 --> 01:30:20.950
you're going to re-injure
your ankle for example,

01:30:20.950 --> 01:30:23.030
or does it simply mean
that your body is healing

01:30:23.030 --> 01:30:24.800
and the pain is a sign that,

01:30:24.800 --> 01:30:27.200
gradually things are
getting back to normal

01:30:27.200 --> 01:30:31.720
and so, you can modify
the way you process pain,

01:30:31.720 --> 01:30:35.490
based on what your brain
tells you the pain means.

01:30:35.490 --> 01:30:37.740
And that's true for
emotional pain as well.

01:30:37.740 --> 01:30:42.060
And particularly where I think
a strategy that really helps,

01:30:42.060 --> 01:30:45.880
is if you think of an
interpersonal problem

01:30:45.880 --> 01:30:50.880
or a threat of something
coming as an opportunity

01:30:51.180 --> 01:30:53.600
to do something to
ameliorate the situation.

01:30:53.600 --> 01:30:56.130
So, it's not just it's happening to you,

01:30:56.130 --> 01:30:59.470
but something that you can
influence and do something about.

01:30:59.470 --> 01:31:04.100
So, it's blending the receptive
with the active response,

01:31:04.100 --> 01:31:05.340
that I think can make a difference.

01:31:05.340 --> 01:31:06.950
So, you try and process it in a way

01:31:06.950 --> 01:31:09.470
that gives you a deeper
understanding of what's happening.

01:31:09.470 --> 01:31:11.770
You face it but you also say,

01:31:11.770 --> 01:31:14.630
this is an opportunity for
me to do something about it.

01:31:14.630 --> 01:31:17.340
And the minute you realistically enha-

01:31:17.340 --> 01:31:19.910
and this doesn't mean,
imagine away a heart attack.

01:31:19.910 --> 01:31:24.530
It means figure out how to
rehabilitate from a heart attack

01:31:24.530 --> 01:31:27.310
or a broken leg or something like that

01:31:27.310 --> 01:31:30.120
in a way that you get as much control

01:31:30.120 --> 01:31:32.640
into the situation as you can.

01:31:32.640 --> 01:31:33.473
- I love it.

01:31:34.960 --> 01:31:35.793
Grief.

01:31:36.790 --> 01:31:39.710
Grief is one of those
states that is very hard

01:31:39.710 --> 01:31:42.800
to remove oneself from.

01:31:42.800 --> 01:31:44.570
And a lot of people asked me,

01:31:44.570 --> 01:31:46.250
how do I deal with grief?

01:31:46.250 --> 01:31:48.880
And I'm not a clinician
so I'm deferring to you.

01:31:48.880 --> 01:31:50.300
- Mm-hm.
- On the one hand,

01:31:50.300 --> 01:31:53.560
actually someone at Stanford
recently came to me and said,

01:31:53.560 --> 01:31:56.090
my mother passed away and I
had a sibling that passed away

01:31:56.090 --> 01:31:57.540
and they were the only people that I had.

01:31:57.540 --> 01:31:59.160
And I'm also living alone

01:31:59.160 --> 01:32:01.180
and I'm challenged with
a number of things.

01:32:01.180 --> 01:32:02.170
And,

01:32:02.170 --> 01:32:04.070
they looked like they
were holding it together,

01:32:04.070 --> 01:32:06.520
- Mm-hm.
- very well, in fact,

01:32:06.520 --> 01:32:08.880
given what they were describing.

01:32:08.880 --> 01:32:09.943
And on the one hand,

01:32:11.750 --> 01:32:13.500
well, I certainly pointed
out that I'm not a clinician,

01:32:13.500 --> 01:32:14.840
but I said on the one hand,

01:32:14.840 --> 01:32:17.630
you could imagine that it
would be necessary and useful

01:32:17.630 --> 01:32:19.060
to go into the grief's state,

01:32:19.060 --> 01:32:20.990
if you want to transition through it.

01:32:20.990 --> 01:32:21.823
- Right.

01:32:22.700 --> 01:32:24.880
- On the other hand there,

01:32:24.880 --> 01:32:27.190
I've heard before that the cathartic model

01:32:27.190 --> 01:32:30.410
of just really diving into an emotion,

01:32:30.410 --> 01:32:32.830
can also be potentially hazardous.

01:32:32.830 --> 01:32:37.830
If you don't have any
anchors to grab onto.

01:32:37.880 --> 01:32:41.730
What is view of psychiatry
or your view of grief

01:32:41.730 --> 01:32:43.040
and how to deal with grief?

01:32:43.040 --> 01:32:44.780
Because I think grief is,

01:32:44.780 --> 01:32:48.000
one of those all encompassing
emotions for many people.

01:32:48.000 --> 01:32:49.480
- Yeah, it is.

01:32:49.480 --> 01:32:52.640
And it's a very important,
natural, necessary stage of life.

01:32:52.640 --> 01:32:55.130
And the reason we have
all these grief rituals

01:32:55.130 --> 01:32:59.380
from burials and memorials and headstones

01:32:59.380 --> 01:33:02.180
and sitting shiva and other
things that people do.

01:33:02.180 --> 01:33:04.460
It's a way of making it real,

01:33:04.460 --> 01:33:08.150
that an incomprehensible
loss has to be comprehended.

01:33:08.150 --> 01:33:10.870
You have to realize that you're
now going to have to live life

01:33:10.870 --> 01:33:14.513
without your loved one, your
parent, your sibling, whoever.

01:33:15.430 --> 01:33:18.310
And we've all gone through
this at one time or another,

01:33:18.310 --> 01:33:19.710
I certainly have.

01:33:19.710 --> 01:33:22.880
And it's very hard to
just come to terms with,

01:33:22.880 --> 01:33:25.993
but one principle is to sort of say,

01:33:27.160 --> 01:33:29.540
it's never all or none, it's more or less.

01:33:29.540 --> 01:33:33.000
So yes, it's all or none
that you've lost a loved one,

01:33:33.000 --> 01:33:35.860
but I ask people as part of their grieving

01:33:35.860 --> 01:33:37.390
to say to themselves,

01:33:37.390 --> 01:33:39.713
and I do this in hypnosis sometimes too.

01:33:41.670 --> 01:33:44.174
You've lost them but what
have they left you with?

01:33:44.174 --> 01:33:47.450
What have they bequeathed to
you even though they're gone?

01:33:47.450 --> 01:33:49.300
And I'll sometimes ask them to say,

01:33:49.300 --> 01:33:51.560
if your mother could be here right now,

01:33:51.560 --> 01:33:53.310
what would she say to you?

01:33:53.310 --> 01:33:55.060
How would she feel about your life now?

01:33:55.060 --> 01:33:57.000
What would she advise you to do?

01:33:57.000 --> 01:33:58.820
So, in our support groups

01:33:58.820 --> 01:34:02.000
for women with advanced
breast cancer, we lost people.

01:34:02.000 --> 01:34:04.830
And I got to tell you that we
were warned by oncologists,

01:34:04.830 --> 01:34:06.690
that we demoralized people that,

01:34:06.690 --> 01:34:08.340
I mean, they were wonderful oncologists,

01:34:08.340 --> 01:34:10.290
but there are some that were very afraid

01:34:10.290 --> 01:34:13.040
that we would harm them in some way,

01:34:13.040 --> 01:34:16.590
because the mortality rate is fairly high

01:34:16.590 --> 01:34:18.290
with metastatic breast cancer.

01:34:18.290 --> 01:34:21.150
They're going to watch people
die of the same disease

01:34:21.150 --> 01:34:23.080
and you'll demoralize them.

01:34:23.080 --> 01:34:25.930
So, we actually measured their emotion

01:34:25.930 --> 01:34:28.230
and the content of
speech every five minutes

01:34:28.230 --> 01:34:29.870
throughout a bunch of groups to make sure

01:34:29.870 --> 01:34:30.703
that wasn't happening.

01:34:30.703 --> 01:34:31.990
What we found was,

01:34:31.990 --> 01:34:33.780
that they talked about
more serious issues,

01:34:33.780 --> 01:34:35.870
but the mood didn't actually get worse.

01:34:35.870 --> 01:34:39.010
And we found in general that
expressing negative emotion

01:34:39.010 --> 01:34:41.190
on the long run helps
people be less anxious

01:34:41.190 --> 01:34:42.140
and depressed over time.

01:34:42.140 --> 01:34:44.350
And we've shown this in
randomized clinical trials.

01:34:44.350 --> 01:34:47.220
So, it's not just my clinical impression.

01:34:47.220 --> 01:34:51.820
And what we try to get them to do,

01:34:51.820 --> 01:34:54.670
is to face a loss,

01:34:54.670 --> 01:34:56.240
live with the emotion that comes with it

01:34:56.240 --> 01:34:58.810
but also see that the
reason it hurts so much,

01:34:58.810 --> 01:35:00.530
is how much that person gave you.

01:35:00.530 --> 01:35:03.110
So, we would do a self-hypnosis exercise

01:35:03.110 --> 01:35:03.943
at the [indistinct] say,

01:35:03.943 --> 01:35:06.990
I want you to get your body
floating, safe and comfortable.

01:35:06.990 --> 01:35:11.520
Now, picture Mary and sit
with the feeling of sadness

01:35:11.520 --> 01:35:13.450
that she's no longer with us.

01:35:13.450 --> 01:35:14.950
And we do that for a few minutes.

01:35:14.950 --> 01:35:16.750
And then we'd say on the other side,

01:35:16.750 --> 01:35:19.640
picture one thing she left
with you that you still have,

01:35:19.640 --> 01:35:21.790
that you carry on in your heart,

01:35:21.790 --> 01:35:24.380
her tradition of what she gave to you.

01:35:24.380 --> 01:35:28.120
And so just seeing it,
not as a complete loss,

01:35:28.120 --> 01:35:30.780
but as a real loss, a painful loss

01:35:30.780 --> 01:35:32.680
but one that helps you to reflect on,

01:35:32.680 --> 01:35:35.730
what you gained from her and knowing her,

01:35:35.730 --> 01:35:38.570
I think can be very helpful
in the grieving process.

01:35:38.570 --> 01:35:42.090
- That's very helpful
way to conceptualize it.

01:35:42.090 --> 01:35:43.573
Couple quick questions.

01:35:44.730 --> 01:35:48.833
Can children be safely
hypnotized or do self-hypnosis?

01:35:50.200 --> 01:35:52.880
- It's sometimes harder for
them to do self-hypnosis,

01:35:52.880 --> 01:35:54.590
they need more structure to do it.

01:35:54.590 --> 01:35:58.390
You've got to share your
dorsolateral prefrontal cortex

01:35:58.390 --> 01:35:59.700
with 'em a little bit,

01:35:59.700 --> 01:36:03.006
but yes, absolutely, children
can be very hypnotizable.

01:36:03.006 --> 01:36:05.950
And I know pediatricians,

01:36:05.950 --> 01:36:08.210
who use it wonderfully all the time.

01:36:08.210 --> 01:36:10.810
They get them to focus on something else.

01:36:10.810 --> 01:36:12.600
So, they're going to
have to give them a shot

01:36:12.600 --> 01:36:14.210
or draw blood or something.

01:36:14.210 --> 01:36:15.610
And they'll say,

01:36:15.610 --> 01:36:18.390
I'm going to press your happy button

01:36:18.390 --> 01:36:20.040
and he presses their belly button.

01:36:20.040 --> 01:36:21.840
And they start to giggle the way kids do.

01:36:21.840 --> 01:36:24.440
And meanwhile, the nurse
is drawing the blood

01:36:24.440 --> 01:36:26.230
and they don't even notice it.

01:36:26.230 --> 01:36:28.950
Dentists, good dentists
can use it to help kids

01:36:28.950 --> 01:36:30.380
with fear and pain.

01:36:30.380 --> 01:36:32.720
So yes, it can be very
effective for children.

01:36:32.720 --> 01:36:34.030
We did a randomized trial.

01:36:34.030 --> 01:36:35.990
I have a publication in pediatrics,

01:36:35.990 --> 01:36:38.003
my late sister, who was a pediatrician,

01:36:38.850 --> 01:36:40.860
and who always used to joke that,

01:36:40.860 --> 01:36:43.710
she was the only one in our
family who was a real doctor.

01:36:44.640 --> 01:36:45.610
I said, I gotcha.

01:36:45.610 --> 01:36:47.940
I got a paper in pediatrics
[Andrew laughing]

01:36:47.940 --> 01:36:51.510
and the paper was children
having to undergo,

01:36:51.510 --> 01:36:53.080
a voiding cystourethrogram.

01:36:53.080 --> 01:36:56.270
So, the anatomy of the kidney,

01:36:56.270 --> 01:36:58.790
if you'll forgive me, is
sort of interesting in that,

01:36:58.790 --> 01:37:01.700
the ureter that goes into the bladder,

01:37:01.700 --> 01:37:04.120
normally goes into the
bladder at an angle.

01:37:04.120 --> 01:37:05.440
And so that means,

01:37:05.440 --> 01:37:08.220
that when the bladder
contracts to expel urine,

01:37:08.220 --> 01:37:10.800
it automatically closes off the ureter

01:37:10.800 --> 01:37:12.830
because it's sideways to the bladder.

01:37:12.830 --> 01:37:14.900
Some kids are born with it perpendicular,

01:37:14.900 --> 01:37:17.160
and then you'll get
reflux into the kidney.

01:37:17.160 --> 01:37:19.260
And some children outgrow it,

01:37:19.260 --> 01:37:22.880
some need pretty complicated
surgery to fix that.

01:37:22.880 --> 01:37:26.020
And so, you image them every year or so

01:37:26.020 --> 01:37:28.680
to see whether they're
getting kidney damage or not.

01:37:28.680 --> 01:37:30.490
And it's a pretty miserable experience.

01:37:30.490 --> 01:37:32.040
You're a nine year old girl.

01:37:32.040 --> 01:37:33.920
You have to go and lie
on a hard cold table,

01:37:33.920 --> 01:37:35.680
have strangers pull your legs apart

01:37:35.680 --> 01:37:38.520
and stick a catheter into your urethra

01:37:38.520 --> 01:37:42.020
and hold in the bladder
and then expel urine.

01:37:42.020 --> 01:37:43.830
And so, you get into
these struggling fights.

01:37:43.830 --> 01:37:45.170
And of course the more they struggle,

01:37:45.170 --> 01:37:47.580
the more they constrict and
it makes it harder to do it.

01:37:47.580 --> 01:37:51.040
So, I was asked if we could test this.

01:37:51.040 --> 01:37:54.130
So, we did a randomized
trial at children's hospital.

01:37:54.130 --> 01:37:56.270
They either got training in self-hypnosis.

01:37:56.270 --> 01:37:59.230
I would meet with them and
the mother the week before,

01:37:59.230 --> 01:38:01.500
we find out from the kids
where they like to be.

01:38:01.500 --> 01:38:03.780
And I'd say, you're going to
play a trick on your doctors.

01:38:03.780 --> 01:38:05.630
Your body's there, you're somewhere else,

01:38:05.630 --> 01:38:08.220
go visit your friend, go to
Disneyland, do something else.

01:38:08.220 --> 01:38:10.100
And the mother would work on this with me

01:38:10.100 --> 01:38:11.640
at the head of the table.

01:38:11.640 --> 01:38:15.320
And we found that these children
were much easier to image.

01:38:15.320 --> 01:38:17.660
One got so relaxed that, so
your guy [indistinct] he's said,

01:38:17.660 --> 01:38:19.850
normally it takes us 10
minutes to get them to pee

01:38:19.850 --> 01:38:21.200
after they're doing this.

01:38:21.200 --> 01:38:22.820
She was so relaxed, she started peeing

01:38:22.820 --> 01:38:25.150
before I could even get the
bed pan under her [laughs].

01:38:25.150 --> 01:38:27.270
And I had to clean up the table.

01:38:27.270 --> 01:38:30.430
And they also, 17 minutes
shorter procedures.

01:38:30.430 --> 01:38:33.850
And that's a long 17
minutes for a little kid.

01:38:33.850 --> 01:38:36.600
So, it can be very
effective with children.

01:38:36.600 --> 01:38:38.510
They're less anxious, they have less pain

01:38:38.510 --> 01:38:42.390
and get through these
difficult procedures very well.

01:38:42.390 --> 01:38:43.630
- That's great.

01:38:43.630 --> 01:38:46.510
Has hypnosis ever been done for couples,

01:38:46.510 --> 01:38:47.580
like couples therapy?

01:38:47.580 --> 01:38:49.900
I'm thinking of pretty much
every clinical setting here.

01:38:49.900 --> 01:38:51.470
Both people have to be hypnotizable,

01:38:51.470 --> 01:38:52.510
of course.
- Mm-hm, yes.

01:38:52.510 --> 01:38:54.060
- But the reason I ask about this,

01:38:54.060 --> 01:38:56.530
is next, I'm going to
ask about psychedelics

01:38:56.530 --> 01:38:58.470
and there's a lot of interest in,

01:38:58.470 --> 01:39:00.553
coordinating states through the use,

01:39:01.435 --> 01:39:04.090
of drugs of different kinds.
- Mm-hm.

01:39:04.090 --> 01:39:05.870
- We actually do this when
we treat depression, right?

01:39:05.870 --> 01:39:08.390
You have a depressed
person with family members

01:39:08.390 --> 01:39:09.360
who are not depressed, and you say,

01:39:09.360 --> 01:39:12.376
well, let's make them all
not depressed [chuckles],

01:39:12.376 --> 01:39:13.630
[David laughing]
right, I mean,

